window.I18N = {"lang": "en", "dict": {"土地にあるもので": "What the Land Provides", "玉里": "Yuli", "8/11 早朝": "August 11, early morning", "花蓮・富里郷": "Fuli Township, Hualien", "六十石山": "Liushishi Mountain", "結構な早朝、私たちは雲の上にいた。": "Very early that morning, we were above the clouds.", "花蓮県富里郷、新興村。台湾で最初のオーガニック農村、と紹介されている村。": "Xinxing Village, Fuli Township, Hualien County. A place introduced as Taiwan’s first organic farming village.", "北嵯峨のような、東北地方のどこかのような、ノスタルジックな農村風景。\n\n早朝の村から、深い山の上に広がる「六十石山」へ。\n\n幻想的な山の頂上付近は、金針花の群生。つぼみを摘んでスープで味わう。台湾のスイスとも呼ばれる空の村で、花を摘む人たちをお見かけした。": "A rural landscape that felt nostalgic: it could have been Kita-Saga in Kyoto, or somewhere in northeastern Japan.\n\nFrom the village in the early morning, we climbed into the mountains to Liushishi Mountain.\n\nNear its otherworldly summit, daylilies grew in great swathes. Their buds are picked for soup. In this village in the sky, sometimes called Taiwan’s Switzerland, we came across people gathering the flowers.", "北嵯峨のような、東北地方のどこかのような、ノスタルジックな農村風景。": "A rural landscape that felt nostalgic: it could have been Kita-Saga in Kyoto, or somewhere in northeastern Japan.", "早朝の村から、深い山の上に広がる「六十石山」へ。": "From the village in the early morning, we climbed into the mountains to Liushishi Mountain.", "幻想的な山の頂上付近は、金針花の群生。つぼみを摘んでスープで味わう。台湾のスイスとも呼ばれる空の村で、花を摘む人たちをお見かけした。": "Near its otherworldly summit, daylilies grew in great swathes. Their buds are picked for soup. In this village in the sky, sometimes called Taiwan’s Switzerland, we came across people gathering the flowers.", "「台湾最初のオーガニック農村」は本人が投稿で書いた説明。": "“Taiwan’s first organic farming village” is how we described it in our social media post.", "雲海の上に出た早朝の宿": "Our accommodation above a sea of clouds in the early morning", "雲海の朝（8/11 早朝・高台の宿）": "A morning above a sea of clouds (early on August 11, at our hillside accommodation)", "金針花の畑で作業する人": "A person working in a daylily field", "六十石山（8/11）": "Liushishi Mountain (August 11)", "霧の中の金針花畑": "Daylily fields in the mist", "六十石山（8/11 早朝）": "Liushishi Mountain (early on August 11)", "山の畑": "A mountain field", "8/11 朝": "August 11, morning", "花蓮・六十石山": "Liushishi Mountain, Hualien", "80歳を超える女性達が、これが生き甲斐と、高山にてお茶を摘む。": "Women over eighty were picking tea high in the mountains. This, they told us, was what gave their lives purpose.", "ちょうどお茶を摘む時期にも重なり、少しお邪魔した。": "It happened to be tea-picking season, too, and we stopped by for a little while.", "そんなお茶は、烏龍茶、白茶、紅茶になっていく。\n\nお茶畑の横にある、メーガンさんの友人のお店でお茶をした。猫のモチと遊ぶ。猫は本当に、どこの国でもふてぶてしく、そして可愛い。": "Those leaves would become oolong, white tea, and black tea.\n\nWe had tea at a shop beside the tea fields, run by a friend of Megan’s. We played with Mochi the cat. Cats really are wonderfully brazen—and adorable—in every country.", "そんなお茶は、烏龍茶、白茶、紅茶になっていく。": "Those leaves would become oolong, white tea, and black tea.", "お茶畑の横にある、メーガンさんの友人のお店でお茶をした。猫のモチと遊ぶ。猫は本当に、どこの国でもふてぶてしく、そして可愛い。": "We had tea at a shop beside the tea fields, run by a friend of Megan’s. We played with Mochi the cat. Cats really are wonderfully brazen—and adorable—in every country.", "頭巾をかぶって茶を摘む人": "A person in a headscarf picking tea", "茶摘み（8/11 07時台）": "Picking tea (August 11, between 07:00 and 08:00)", "笊に並ぶ茶葉": "Tea leaves laid out on a bamboo tray", "笊に並ぶ茶葉（8/11 07:54）": "Tea leaves laid out on a bamboo tray (August 11, 07:54)", "店の前に立つ旅の仲間": "Fellow travelers standing in front of the shop", "お茶畑の横の店（8/11）": "The shop beside the tea fields (August 11)", "8/11 午前": "August 11, morning", "花蓮・羅山村": "Luoshan Village, Hualien", "羅山村": "Luoshan Village", "昔は陸の孤島だったという羅山村。": "Luoshan Village, once so isolated it was like an island on land.", "山を下りて、羅山村。": "Down the mountain, to Luoshan Village.", "ここでは、村にある「羅山泥火山」から湧き出る水を、豆腐のにがりとして使って来ました。\n\n石臼で大豆を挽いて豆乳に。火を入れて、おからを絞って、もう一度火にかける。\n\nそこに泥火山から汲んできて泥を沈澱させた水を加えると、豆腐が少しずつ固まっていく。\n\nこの豆腐が、まず普通に木綿豆腐のようで美味しい。辛いソースでいただく。豆の味が濃くて、しっかり硬い。\n\nそして、豆腐乳（とうふるー）にするプロセスでは、この豆腐を塩漬けにする。そして蒸すか茹でた後に天日で干す。瓶に入れて、米麹、酒、砂糖などと一緒に発酵させる。そうして3ヶ月経つと、豆腐乳となる。\n\nこれは、そのまま食べてかなり美味しい。\nお粥のお供にするのが、こちらでは定番だそうですが、酒のつまみですね、完全に。\n沖縄の豆腐ように、近い。\n\nそこで、村にある泥火山の水を使って豆腐をつくり、その豆腐を保存食にしていく。": "Here, people have long used water bubbling up from the village’s Luoshan mud volcano as a coagulant for tofu.\n\nSoybeans are ground in a stone mill to make soy milk. Heat it, strain out the pulp, then heat it again.\n\nAdd water drawn from the mud volcano, after letting the mud settle out, and the tofu slowly begins to set.\n\nThe tofu was delicious just as it was, much like ordinary firm Japanese tofu. We ate it with a spicy sauce. A strong soybean flavor and a good, firm texture.\n\nTo make doufuru, fermented tofu, this tofu is salted, then steamed or boiled and dried in the sun. It goes into jars with rice koji, alcohol, sugar, and other ingredients to ferment. After three months, it becomes doufuru.\n\nIt is very good eaten on its own.\nHere, we were told, it is usually served with rice porridge. To us, it was clearly something to eat with a drink.\nQuite close to Okinawa’s fermented tofu, tofuyo.\n\nSo people made tofu with water from the village’s own mud volcano, then turned that tofu into food that would keep.", "ここでは、村にある「羅山泥火山」から湧き出る水を、豆腐のにがりとして使って来ました。": "Here, people have long used water bubbling up from the village’s Luoshan mud volcano as a coagulant for tofu.", "石臼で大豆を挽いて豆乳に。火を入れて、おからを絞って、もう一度火にかける。": "Soybeans are ground in a stone mill to make soy milk. Heat it, strain out the pulp, then heat it again.", "そこに泥火山から汲んできて泥を沈澱させた水を加えると、豆腐が少しずつ固まっていく。": "Add water drawn from the mud volcano, after letting the mud settle out, and the tofu slowly begins to set.", "この豆腐が、まず普通に木綿豆腐のようで美味しい。辛いソースでいただく。豆の味が濃くて、しっかり硬い。": "The tofu was delicious just as it was, much like ordinary firm Japanese tofu. We ate it with a spicy sauce. A strong soybean flavor and a good, firm texture.", "そして、豆腐乳（とうふるー）にするプロセスでは、この豆腐を塩漬けにする。そして蒸すか茹でた後に天日で干す。瓶に入れて、米麹、酒、砂糖などと一緒に発酵させる。そうして3ヶ月経つと、豆腐乳となる。": "To make doufuru, fermented tofu, this tofu is salted, then steamed or boiled and dried in the sun. It goes into jars with rice koji, alcohol, sugar, and other ingredients to ferment. After three months, it becomes doufuru.", "これは、そのまま食べてかなり美味しい。": "It is very good eaten on its own.", "お粥のお供にするのが、こちらでは定番だそうですが、酒のつまみですね、完全に。": "Here, we were told, it is usually served with rice porridge. To us, it was clearly something to eat with a drink.", "沖縄の豆腐ように、近い。": "Quite close to Okinawa’s fermented tofu, tofuyo.", "そこで、村にある泥火山の水を使って豆腐をつくり、その豆腐を保存食にしていく。": "So people made tofu with water from the village’s own mud volcano, then turned that tofu into food that would keep.", "天日で2〜4日干したあと、瓶で約3ヶ月漬ける。": "Two to four days of sun-drying are followed by about three months in jars.", "豆乳を鍋に入れる人": "A person pouring soy milk into a pot", "搾る（8/11 09:54）": "Straining (August 11, 09:54)", "豆腐を搾る人": "A person pressing tofu", "固める（8/11 10:07）": "Setting (August 11, 10:07)", "泥火山豆腐乳の売り場": "Mud-volcano fermented tofu for sale", "土産物店のPOP「泥火山豆腐乳 450g 380元」": "A sign in the gift shop: “Mud-volcano fermented tofu, 450 g, NT$380”", "豆腐づくりの作業場": "The tofu-making workshop", "羅山村（8/11）": "Luoshan Village (August 11)", "豆腐の店の看板": "The tofu shop’s sign", "8/11 午後": "August 11, afternoon", "台東への道": "The road to Taitung", "東海岸（地域表示）": "East coast (general area)", "パイナップルは地面から生える。木から吊り下がるものだと思っていた": "Pineapples grow out of the ground. I thought they hung from trees.", "午後は台東の街まで下り、コーヒーを飲んだ。": "In the afternoon, we headed down into Taitung and had coffee.", "東海岸の焙煎所で10杯以上を飲み比べた。店名は本人の意向で記さない。エチオピア・ゲシャ村の豆の水出しには「ウイスキーのような風味まであり、その香りは、甘さと絡み合ってすっと消える」（投稿原文）。": "We tasted more than ten coffees at a roastery on the east coast, which, at the author’s wish, we leave unnamed. Of the cold brew made with beans from Gesha Village in Ethiopia, the original social media post said: “There is even a whisky-like flavor; the aroma mingles with the sweetness and then slips away.”", "畑に育つパイナップル": "Pineapples growing in a field", "パイナップル畑（8/11 18:26）": "A pineapple field (August 11, 18:26)", "コーヒーを抽出する人": "A person brewing coffee", "東海岸の焙煎所（8/11）": "A roastery on the east coast (August 11)", "8/11 夜": "August 11, evening", "台東": "Taitung", "初めての料理なのに、なぜかしっくり来る。": "We had never eaten these dishes before, yet somehow they felt right.", "夜は台東の街、原住民族料理の店「九格浪」へ。": "That evening, we went to Jiugelang (九格浪), a restaurant serving Indigenous food in Taitung.", "川魚、青菜の炒め物、葉に包まれたもち米。\nそれから、豚肉を塩に漬けて発酵させた「Siraw（シラウ）」の焼肉。": "River fish, stir-fried greens, sticky rice wrapped in leaves.\nThen grilled siraw: pork fermented with salt.", "川魚、青菜の炒め物、葉に包まれたもち米。": "River fish, stir-fried greens, sticky rice wrapped in leaves.", "それから、豚肉を塩に漬けて発酵させた「Siraw（シラウ）」の焼肉。": "Then grilled siraw: pork fermented with salt.", "原住民族料理の食卓にあるスープ": "Soup on a table of Indigenous food", "夜の食卓（8/11 19時台）": "The evening table (August 11, between 19:00 and 20:00)", "原住民族料理店の入口": "The entrance to the Indigenous restaurant", "九格浪（8/11 夜）": "Jiugelang (August 11, evening)", "原住民族料理の食卓": "A table of Indigenous food", "夜の食卓（8/11）": "The evening table (August 11)", "値段のないスーパー": "A Supermarket Without Prices", "8/12 朝": "August 12, morning", "台東・樹屋廣場": "Tree House Plaza, Taitung", "樹屋廣場": "Tree House Plaza", "「私たちは、終わりなきものを作り上げる為に『待つ』ということに、どれだけの時間を費やせるだろうか？」": "“How much time can we give to waiting, in order to create something without an end?”", "台東の街中で、博覧会をやっていた。コンセプトは「SLOW FOR LIFE」。": "There was an expo in central Taitung. Its theme was “SLOW FOR LIFE.”", "「ゆっくり暮らそう」という呼びかけではなく、台東の風土の豊かさ、古くから暮らした人々の知恵、そして時間そのものを価値として捉え直す。\n\nそのなかの一つが、スーパーマーケットの形をしている。\n買い物籠を持って、棚を巡る。棚に並んでいるのは、商品ではない。台東の植物、穀物、肉、酒。\n\nガラス越しに見るのではなく、蓋を開けて匂いを嗅ぐ。実物に触れる。映像で見る、聴く。一部は食べる。\n\n特に印象に残ったのが二つ。": "It was about more than an invitation to slow down. It asked us to recognize the richness of Taitung’s land and way of life, the knowledge of the people who had lived there for generations, and the value of time itself.\n\nOne of the exhibitions took the form of a supermarket.\nYou picked up a basket and walked the aisles. The shelves held no merchandise: they held Taitung’s plants, grains, meat, and wine.\n\nInstead of looking through glass, you lifted a lid and smelled what was inside. You touched the real thing. Watched and listened to films. Tasted some of it, too.\n\nTwo things stayed with us in particular.", "「ゆっくり暮らそう」という呼びかけではなく、台東の風土の豊かさ、古くから暮らした人々の知恵、そして時間そのものを価値として捉え直す。": "It was about more than an invitation to slow down. It asked us to recognize the richness of Taitung’s land and way of life, the knowledge of the people who had lived there for generations, and the value of time itself.", "そのなかの一つが、スーパーマーケットの形をしている。": "One of the exhibitions took the form of a supermarket.", "買い物籠を持って、棚を巡る。棚に並んでいるのは、商品ではない。台東の植物、穀物、肉、酒。": "You picked up a basket and walked the aisles. The shelves held no merchandise: they held Taitung’s plants, grains, meat, and wine.", "ガラス越しに見るのではなく、蓋を開けて匂いを嗅ぐ。実物に触れる。映像で見る、聴く。一部は食べる。": "Instead of looking through glass, you lifted a lid and smelled what was inside. You touched the real thing. Watched and listened to films. Tasted some of it, too.", "特に印象に残ったのが二つ。": "Two things stayed with us in particular.", "2026台東博覧會 A2《這超台東：未來超市》。": "2026 Taitung Expo, A2 exhibition, “So Taitung: Future Supermarket” (這超台東：未來超市).", "展示棚に並ぶ発酵食品": "Fermented foods on display shelves", "棚と籠（8/12 10:00〜10:16 樹屋廣場）": "Shelves and baskets (Tree House Plaza, August 12, 10:00–10:16)", "展示会場の棚": "Shelves in the exhibition space", "樹屋廣場（8/12）": "Tree House Plaza (August 12)", "展示棚に並ぶ容器": "Containers on display shelves", "同じ「シラウ」でも、つくる人と土地によって違う。": "It is all called siraw, yet it changes with the people and the place.", "ひとつ目は、アミ族の伝統発酵肉「Siraw（シラウ）」。": "The first was siraw, the traditional fermented meat of the Amis people.", "シラウは、アミ族に伝わる伝統的な肉の保存・発酵食で、昨晩少しだけ焼いたものを食べたけれど、生を見るのははじめて。\n棚には、何種類かのシラウが並んでいた。\n\n肉に塩を加え、時間をかけて熟成させていくことだけが共通しているものの、部落や家庭によって、つくり方や味わいが異なるそうです。\n\n塩と時間によって肉の水分を抜き、旨味を凝縮させるもの。\n\n肉汁を残しながら熟成させるもの。\n\nご飯を加えるもの。これはふなずしに近いかもしれない。\n\nそして、もち米の酒粕と米酒を加えて、芳醇な香りをまとわせるもの。": "Siraw is a way of preserving and fermenting meat handed down among the Amis. We had eaten a little of it grilled the night before, but this was our first time seeing it uncooked.\nSeveral kinds were arranged on the shelves.\n\nThey all involve adding salt to meat and leaving it to age, but we were told that methods and flavors vary from village to village and household to household.\n\nSome use salt and time to draw out moisture and concentrate the savory flavor.\n\nSome retain the meat’s juices as it ages.\n\nSome include cooked rice. These might be rather like funazushi, the fish fermented with rice in Japan.\n\nOthers include glutinous-rice wine lees and rice wine, giving the meat a rich aroma.", "シラウは、アミ族に伝わる伝統的な肉の保存・発酵食で、昨晩少しだけ焼いたものを食べたけれど、生を見るのははじめて。": "Siraw is a way of preserving and fermenting meat handed down among the Amis. We had eaten a little of it grilled the night before, but this was our first time seeing it uncooked.", "棚には、何種類かのシラウが並んでいた。": "Several kinds were arranged on the shelves.", "肉に塩を加え、時間をかけて熟成させていくことだけが共通しているものの、部落や家庭によって、つくり方や味わいが異なるそうです。": "They all involve adding salt to meat and leaving it to age, but we were told that methods and flavors vary from village to village and household to household.", "塩と時間によって肉の水分を抜き、旨味を凝縮させるもの。": "Some use salt and time to draw out moisture and concentrate the savory flavor.", "肉汁を残しながら熟成させるもの。": "Some retain the meat’s juices as it ages.", "ご飯を加えるもの。これはふなずしに近いかもしれない。": "Some include cooked rice. These might be rather like funazushi, the fish fermented with rice in Japan.", "そして、もち米の酒粕と米酒を加えて、芳醇な香りをまとわせるもの。": "Others include glutinous-rice wine lees and rice wine, giving the meat a rich aroma.", "棚に並ぶ何種類かのシラウ": "Several kinds of siraw on a shelf", "Siraw実物の展示（8/12 09:56）": "Real siraw on display (August 12, 09:56)", "瓶に並ぶSirawの展示": "Siraw displayed in jars", "しかも、使う植物は部落によって違う。": "And the plants used differ from village to village.", "二つ目は、原住民の醸す酒。": "The second was the wine brewed by Indigenous peoples.", "以前、發酵迷のメーガンさんからもらった台湾原住民族のお酒、小米酒（シャオミージョウ）。\n日本酒の水酛造りの酒を思わせるような不思議な美味しさ、かつかなりのアルコール感があって、迫力あり。\n\n「原住民族の酒って、こんな酒なんだ」くらいだったけど、今回、原住民の「醸造文化」に触れることができた。\n\n台湾原住民族・阿美族の酒は、もち米や小米（粟）などを、土地の植物からつくった酒麴で醸す。\n\n面白いのは、酒づくりが「酒を仕込むところ」から始まらないこと。\n\n部落の周囲の植物を採集し、搗いて汁を取り、穀物の粉などと混ぜて丸め、葉で覆って発酵させ、乾燥させて酒麴にする。\n\n大葉田香、荖葉、毛柿、七里香、山素英、過山香、艾草、野菊、九層塔……": "Megan from Fajiaomi had once given us a Taiwanese Indigenous millet wine, xiaomijiu.\nIt had an intriguing flavor that reminded us of sake made with the mizumoto starter method, and a decidedly powerful alcoholic kick.\n\nAt the time, we had thought little beyond, “So this is what Indigenous wine is like.” On this visit, we began to encounter the brewing culture behind it.\n\nThe Amis, one of Taiwan’s Indigenous peoples, brew glutinous rice, foxtail millet, and other grains with a fermentation starter made using local plants.\n\nWhat interested us was that brewing did not begin with putting a batch of wine together.\n\nFirst, plants are gathered around the village and pounded to extract their juices. These are mixed with grain flour and other ingredients, shaped into balls, covered with leaves, fermented, and dried to make the starter.\n\nDayetianxiang, laoye, maoshi, qilixiang, shansuying, guoshanxiang, aicao, yeju, jiucengta…", "以前、發酵迷のメーガンさんからもらった台湾原住民族のお酒、小米酒（シャオミージョウ）。": "Megan from Fajiaomi had once given us a Taiwanese Indigenous millet wine, xiaomijiu.", "日本酒の水酛造りの酒を思わせるような不思議な美味しさ、かつかなりのアルコール感があって、迫力あり。": "It had an intriguing flavor that reminded us of sake made with the mizumoto starter method, and a decidedly powerful alcoholic kick.", "「原住民族の酒って、こんな酒なんだ」くらいだったけど、今回、原住民の「醸造文化」に触れることができた。": "At the time, we had thought little beyond, “So this is what Indigenous wine is like.” On this visit, we began to encounter the brewing culture behind it.", "台湾原住民族・阿美族の酒は、もち米や小米（粟）などを、土地の植物からつくった酒麴で醸す。": "The Amis, one of Taiwan’s Indigenous peoples, brew glutinous rice, foxtail millet, and other grains with a fermentation starter made using local plants.", "面白いのは、酒づくりが「酒を仕込むところ」から始まらないこと。": "What interested us was that brewing did not begin with putting a batch of wine together.", "部落の周囲の植物を採集し、搗いて汁を取り、穀物の粉などと混ぜて丸め、葉で覆って発酵させ、乾燥させて酒麴にする。": "First, plants are gathered around the village and pounded to extract their juices. These are mixed with grain flour and other ingredients, shaped into balls, covered with leaves, fermented, and dried to make the starter.", "大葉田香、荖葉、毛柿、七里香、山素英、過山香、艾草、野菊、九層塔……": "Dayetianxiang, laoye, maoshi, qilixiang, shansuying, guoshanxiang, aicao, yeju, jiucengta…", "植物名は展示にそう書かれていたもの（投稿原文）。植物学的な照合は行っていない。": "These are the plant names as shown in the exhibition, recorded in the original social media post. Their botanical identities have not been verified.", "伝統穀物酒の展示": "Display of traditional grain wine", "「傳統穀物酒 Traditional Grain Wine」の壺": "A jar labeled “傳統穀物酒 Traditional Grain Wine”", "穀物と酒の展示": "Display of grains and wine", "8/12 昼": "August 12, midday", "渡されたのは、レシートではなかった。": "What we received was not a receipt.", "棚を回り終えると、レジがある。": "At the end of the aisles, there was a checkout.", "渡されるのは、値段の書かれたレシートではない。種を守ってきた物語、熟成にかかった時間、料理の方法。\n\n受け取って、ちょっと見て、よくわからずに、ポケットにくしゃっと入れてしまった。\n\n昼、街の防空訓練に遭遇して、ハンバーガー屋に退避した。これも台湾のリアル。": "What you received was not a receipt listing prices, but stories of seeds kept alive, time spent aging, and ways of cooking.\n\nI took mine, glanced at it, failed to make much sense of it, and crumpled it into my pocket.\n\nAround lunchtime, we found ourselves in the middle of an air-raid drill and took shelter in a burger shop. This, too, was part of life in Taiwan.", "渡されるのは、値段の書かれたレシートではない。種を守ってきた物語、熟成にかかった時間、料理の方法。": "What you received was not a receipt listing prices, but stories of seeds kept alive, time spent aging, and ways of cooking.", "受け取って、ちょっと見て、よくわからずに、ポケットにくしゃっと入れてしまった。": "I took mine, glanced at it, failed to make much sense of it, and crumpled it into my pocket.", "昼、街の防空訓練に遭遇して、ハンバーガー屋に退避した。これも台湾のリアル。": "Around lunchtime, we found ourselves in the middle of an air-raid drill and took shelter in a burger shop. This, too, was part of life in Taiwan.", "「風土收據」。": "The “receipt of the land” (風土收據).", "まだ気に入っていません": "“I’m Not Happy with It Yet”", "8/13 昼": "August 13, midday", "屏東・皇珵醬油": "Royaljade Brewing, Pingtung", "皇珵醬油": "Royaljade Brewing", "台湾一周で一番おいしかった食事。母の味が強し。": "The best meal of the entire trip. Hard to beat a mother’s cooking.", "「お昼はここで食事をします。その間にゆっくりと醤油の紹介をしていきます」": "“We’ll have lunch here. While we eat, I’ll take my time introducing the soy sauces.”", "電車で、台東から南へ回り込む。\n台湾の友人が市場で買って持たせてくれたマンゴーを、台東駅の前のベンチで食べた。切っただけ。それが美味しかった。\n\n台湾の南、屏東の田んぼのなかにある醤油蔵、皇珵醬油（Royaljade Brewing）を訪ねました。\n\nこれから台南を案内してくれるメーガンさんの生徒「天天」さんの、お母さんの料理だった。屏東の家庭料理。\n\n麻油雞（マーヨージー）。\n台湾特有のごま油で生姜をよく炒めて、台湾の酒と、鶏。冬に体を温める料理で、産後によく食べるらしい。\n\n「なんか、これは新しいっていうより懐かしい味だよね、これは」\n「秋田のきりたんぽに、ちょっと近いかも」\n\n食後に外に出た。": "By train, we traveled south from Taitung, rounding the southern end of the island.\nOn a bench outside Taitung Station, we ate mangoes a Taiwanese friend had bought at the market and sent us off with. Simply cut up. So good.\n\nWe visited Royaljade Brewing (皇珵醬油), a soy sauce brewery among the rice fields of Pingtung, in southern Taiwan.\n\nThe food was cooked by the mother of Tiantian, one of Megan’s students, who would be showing us around Tainan. Home cooking from Pingtung.\n\nMa you ji: sesame-oil chicken.\nGinger thoroughly fried in the distinctive sesame oil used in Taiwan, then Taiwanese rice wine and chicken. A warming winter dish, often eaten after childbirth, we were told.\n\n“Somehow this tastes familiar, rather than new.”\n“A little like kiritanpo from Akita, perhaps.”\n\nAfter lunch, we went outside.", "電車で、台東から南へ回り込む。": "By train, we traveled south from Taitung, rounding the southern end of the island.", "台湾の友人が市場で買って持たせてくれたマンゴーを、台東駅の前のベンチで食べた。切っただけ。それが美味しかった。": "On a bench outside Taitung Station, we ate mangoes a Taiwanese friend had bought at the market and sent us off with. Simply cut up. So good.", "台湾の南、屏東の田んぼのなかにある醤油蔵、皇珵醬油（Royaljade Brewing）を訪ねました。": "We visited Royaljade Brewing (皇珵醬油), a soy sauce brewery among the rice fields of Pingtung, in southern Taiwan.", "これから台南を案内してくれるメーガンさんの生徒「天天」さんの、お母さんの料理だった。屏東の家庭料理。": "The food was cooked by the mother of Tiantian, one of Megan’s students, who would be showing us around Tainan. Home cooking from Pingtung.", "麻油雞（マーヨージー）。": "Ma you ji: sesame-oil chicken.", "台湾特有のごま油で生姜をよく炒めて、台湾の酒と、鶏。冬に体を温める料理で、産後によく食べるらしい。": "Ginger thoroughly fried in the distinctive sesame oil used in Taiwan, then Taiwanese rice wine and chicken. A warming winter dish, often eaten after childbirth, we were told.", "「なんか、これは新しいっていうより懐かしい味だよね、これは」": "“Somehow this tastes familiar, rather than new.”", "「秋田のきりたんぽに、ちょっと近いかも」": "“A little like kiritanpo from Akita, perhaps.”", "食後に外に出た。": "After lunch, we went outside.", "手に持った麻油雞のスープ": "A bowl of sesame-oil chicken soup held in a hand", "麻油雞（8/13 昼）": "Sesame-oil chicken (August 13, midday)", "屏東の家庭料理の食卓": "A table of Pingtung home cooking", "食卓（8/13 昼）": "The table (August 13, midday)", "甕の上に置かれたカモシカ氏のぬいぐるみ": "A Kamoshika-shi plush toy sitting on a jar", "甕の上のカモシカ氏（8/13）": "Kamoshika-shi on a jar (August 13)", "8/13 午後": "August 13, afternoon", "「醤油を学ぶ過程で、日本の分類が原料の比率で分かれていることに気づきました。台湾には、それがない」": "“As I studied soy sauce, I realized that the Japanese categories were based on the proportions of the ingredients. Taiwan doesn’t have that.”", "資料室に入って、まず目に入ったのが七本の試験管。": "The first thing we noticed in the display room was a row of seven test tubes.", "作り手は、元ITエンジニアの簡志斌（ジエン・ジービン）さん。\n\n実家は米農家。米の値段があまりに安かったことと、日本酒が好きだったことから、最初は清酒をつくりたかったそうです。\n\nでも、十数年前の台湾には酒米がなくて断念。\n\nそこから醤油づくりに入り、そのままハマっていった。\n\n中身は醤油の原料の可視化です。\n\n青い粉は水の分量、白い粒は塩の分量。一本ごとに穀物の配合も違っていて、一本がそのまま一つの醤油のレシピになっています。\n\nそのうち五本は、日本の醤油の分類。\n濃口、淡口、たまり、白、再仕込み。\n\n簡さんは独学で、日本醤油協会や日本醸造協会の資料をネットで調べ、種麹も日本から取り寄せて試したそう。\n\nそして、残りの二本が台湾の黒豆。\n台湾には黒豆で醤油を仕込む伝統がある。\nそれを甘めに調整する。\n\n黒豆は大量生産には向かないから、甕に入れて天日に置き、時間をかける昔ながらの仕込みが残っているそうです。鹿児島の黒酢の仕込み風景のミニ版のように感じた。\n\n黒豆の、再仕込みは、日本にも、台湾の伝統にもなかったものです。日本から学んだ分類と、台湾で受け継がれてきた黒豆。組み合わせて、自分の醤油をつくっている。\n\n麹の選び方も面白かったです。\n以前は黒麹を使っていたけれど、やめた、と。\n「だって、最後には部屋中に他より強い黒麹が広がって、他の麹が死んでしまう」": "The maker is Jian Zhibin (簡志斌), a former IT engineer.\n\nHis family grows rice. With rice prices so low, and his own fondness for sake, he had originally wanted to make sake.\n\nBut this was more than a decade ago, and there was no sake-brewing rice in Taiwan. He gave up the idea.\n\nFrom there he turned to soy sauce, and became absorbed in it.\n\nTheir contents made the ingredients of soy sauce visible.\n\nBlue powder represented the amount of water; white granules, the salt. The grain proportions differed in each tube, so that each one was a complete soy sauce recipe.\n\nFive represented the Japanese categories:\nkoikuchi (dark), usukuchi (light-colored), tamari, shiro (white), and saishikomi (double-brewed).\n\nJian taught himself, researching materials from the Japan Soy Sauce Association and the Brewing Society of Japan online. He also ordered koji starter cultures from Japan to experiment with.\n\nThe other two tubes contained Taiwanese black soybeans.\nTaiwan has a tradition of brewing soy sauce from black soybeans.\nThese sauces are adjusted to give them a sweeter taste.\n\nBlack soybeans do not lend themselves to mass production, he told us, so the old method survives: put them in earthenware jars, leave them in the sun, and give them time. It reminded us of a smaller version of the black-vinegar brewing yards in Kagoshima.\n\nDouble-brewed black-soybean sauce was something neither Japan nor Taiwan’s tradition had offered. He was bringing together the categories he had learned from Japan and the black soybeans handed down through Taiwanese practice, making a soy sauce of his own.\n\nHis choice of koji was interesting, too.\nHe used to use black koji mold, he said, but had stopped.\n“In the end, the black koji takes over the whole room. It’s stronger than the others, and the other koji molds die.”", "作り手は、元ITエンジニアの簡志斌（ジエン・ジービン）さん。": "The maker is Jian Zhibin (簡志斌), a former IT engineer.", "実家は米農家。米の値段があまりに安かったことと、日本酒が好きだったことから、最初は清酒をつくりたかったそうです。": "His family grows rice. With rice prices so low, and his own fondness for sake, he had originally wanted to make sake.", "でも、十数年前の台湾には酒米がなくて断念。": "But this was more than a decade ago, and there was no sake-brewing rice in Taiwan. He gave up the idea.", "そこから醤油づくりに入り、そのままハマっていった。": "From there he turned to soy sauce, and became absorbed in it.", "中身は醤油の原料の可視化です。": "Their contents made the ingredients of soy sauce visible.", "青い粉は水の分量、白い粒は塩の分量。一本ごとに穀物の配合も違っていて、一本がそのまま一つの醤油のレシピになっています。": "Blue powder represented the amount of water; white granules, the salt. The grain proportions differed in each tube, so that each one was a complete soy sauce recipe.", "そのうち五本は、日本の醤油の分類。": "Five represented the Japanese categories:", "濃口、淡口、たまり、白、再仕込み。": "koikuchi (dark), usukuchi (light-colored), tamari, shiro (white), and saishikomi (double-brewed).", "簡さんは独学で、日本醤油協会や日本醸造協会の資料をネットで調べ、種麹も日本から取り寄せて試したそう。": "Jian taught himself, researching materials from the Japan Soy Sauce Association and the Brewing Society of Japan online. He also ordered koji starter cultures from Japan to experiment with.", "そして、残りの二本が台湾の黒豆。": "The other two tubes contained Taiwanese black soybeans.", "台湾には黒豆で醤油を仕込む伝統がある。": "Taiwan has a tradition of brewing soy sauce from black soybeans.", "それを甘めに調整する。": "These sauces are adjusted to give them a sweeter taste.", "黒豆は大量生産には向かないから、甕に入れて天日に置き、時間をかける昔ながらの仕込みが残っているそうです。鹿児島の黒酢の仕込み風景のミニ版のように感じた。": "Black soybeans do not lend themselves to mass production, he told us, so the old method survives: put them in earthenware jars, leave them in the sun, and give them time. It reminded us of a smaller version of the black-vinegar brewing yards in Kagoshima.", "黒豆の、再仕込みは、日本にも、台湾の伝統にもなかったものです。日本から学んだ分類と、台湾で受け継がれてきた黒豆。組み合わせて、自分の醤油をつくっている。": "Double-brewed black-soybean sauce was something neither Japan nor Taiwan’s tradition had offered. He was bringing together the categories he had learned from Japan and the black soybeans handed down through Taiwanese practice, making a soy sauce of his own.", "麹の選び方も面白かったです。": "His choice of koji was interesting, too.", "以前は黒麹を使っていたけれど、やめた、と。": "He used to use black koji mold, he said, but had stopped.", "「だって、最後には部屋中に他より強い黒麹が広がって、他の麹が死んでしまう」": "“In the end, the black koji takes over the whole room. It’s stronger than the others, and the other koji molds die.”", "七本の試験管": "Seven test tubes", "七本の試験管（8/13 11:33）": "Seven test tubes (August 13, 11:33)", "醤油蔵の麹室": "The koji room at the soy sauce brewery", "皇珵醬油（8/13）": "Royaljade Brewing (August 13)", "「この製品は、まだ私は気に入っていません」": "“I’m not happy with this product yet.”", "外に出ると、素焼きの甕がずらり。": "Outside stood rows of unglazed earthenware jars.", "台湾でこの甕をつくれる老職人は、もう二人しかいないそうです。注文すると二、三年待ち。手づくりの甕は薄くて軽い。\n\n毎日混ぜる甕には軽い笠を、混ぜ終わったものには重い蓋を載せる。\n\n最後に、実験中だという海藻の醤油の製造工程と味見もさせてもらいました。\n一年もの。\n少し漁港のような香りがして、貝のような旨みがある。\n\nえっ、と思ったら、\n「海藻での醤油作りはまだ始まったばかりですから」\nとのこと。\n\n蔵もレシピも材料も手作りするご夫婦は、とても謙虚に、古い文化の土台に現代的感性と絶え間ない努力を重ねていた。\n\nカモシカとしては「白醤油」が、鮎魚醤のような綺麗な風味としっかりした味で、一番好みだった。翌朝、アボカドにかけただけで食べた。これがまたうまいのなんの。\n\nいつかご家族で京都に来てくれるかな。\n\n夜、台南。": "Only two elderly craftspeople in Taiwan could still make them, we were told. An order meant a wait of two or three years. The handmade jars were thin-walled and light.\n\nThe jars still being stirred every day wore lightweight, hat-shaped covers. Those past the stirring stage had heavy lids.\n\nFinally, we were shown the process for an experimental seaweed soy sauce and invited to taste it.\nOne year old.\nA faint smell of a fishing harbor, with a shellfish-like savoriness.\n\nThat surprised us.\n“We’ve only just begun making soy sauce from seaweed,”\nhe explained.\n\nThe couple made everything by hand: the brewery, the recipes, the ingredients. With great humility, they were adding a contemporary sensibility and unceasing effort to the foundations of an old culture.\n\nOur favorite at Kamoshika was the white soy sauce: a clean flavor like fish sauce made from ayu sweetfish, with plenty of depth. The next morning, we simply poured some over avocado. It was unbelievably good.\n\nPerhaps one day they’ll bring the family to Kyoto.\n\nThat night, Tainan.", "台湾でこの甕をつくれる老職人は、もう二人しかいないそうです。注文すると二、三年待ち。手づくりの甕は薄くて軽い。": "Only two elderly craftspeople in Taiwan could still make them, we were told. An order meant a wait of two or three years. The handmade jars were thin-walled and light.", "毎日混ぜる甕には軽い笠を、混ぜ終わったものには重い蓋を載せる。": "The jars still being stirred every day wore lightweight, hat-shaped covers. Those past the stirring stage had heavy lids.", "最後に、実験中だという海藻の醤油の製造工程と味見もさせてもらいました。": "Finally, we were shown the process for an experimental seaweed soy sauce and invited to taste it.", "一年もの。": "One year old.", "少し漁港のような香りがして、貝のような旨みがある。": "A faint smell of a fishing harbor, with a shellfish-like savoriness.", "えっ、と思ったら、": "That surprised us.", "「海藻での醤油作りはまだ始まったばかりですから」": "“We’ve only just begun making soy sauce from seaweed,”", "とのこと。": "he explained.", "蔵もレシピも材料も手作りするご夫婦は、とても謙虚に、古い文化の土台に現代的感性と絶え間ない努力を重ねていた。": "The couple made everything by hand: the brewery, the recipes, the ingredients. With great humility, they were adding a contemporary sensibility and unceasing effort to the foundations of an old culture.", "カモシカとしては「白醤油」が、鮎魚醤のような綺麗な風味としっかりした味で、一番好みだった。翌朝、アボカドにかけただけで食べた。これがまたうまいのなんの。": "Our favorite at Kamoshika was the white soy sauce: a clean flavor like fish sauce made from ayu sweetfish, with plenty of depth. The next morning, we simply poured some over avocado. It was unbelievably good.", "いつかご家族で京都に来てくれるかな。": "Perhaps one day they’ll bring the family to Kyoto.", "夜、台南。": "That night, Tainan.", "台湾海洋大学の教授との共同試作。": "A joint trial with a professor at National Taiwan Ocean University.", "素焼きの甕": "Unglazed earthenware jars", "素焼きの甕がずらり（8/13 14:04）": "Rows of unglazed earthenware jars (August 13, 14:04)", "甕に載せた笠と蓋": "A light cover and a heavy lid on the jars", "笠と蓋（8/13）": "Light covers and heavy lids (August 13)", "白醤油の瓶": "A bottle of white soy sauce", "白醤油（8/13）": "White soy sauce (August 13)", "8/14 朝": "August 14, morning", "台南": "Tainan", "「このお茶は中国で生まれて、日本で四十年暮らして、そして今、台湾に帰ってきたんです」": "“This tea was born in China, spent forty years living in Japan, and has now come back to Taiwan.”", "雨の台南。": "Rain in Tainan.", "台南で、四十五年もののプーアル茶をいただきました。1981年、中国国営の茶工場でつくられた、最初のひと山。それが日本に渡って、九州で四十年のあいだ保管されていた。\n\n生まれた場所には戻らず、隣の島に戻ってきた。\n\n「どうしてこのお茶を飲んでほしいと思ったか。日本で、とてもきれいに保管されていたからです。悪い味がまったくついていない」\n\n日本から来た私たちに、日本で四十年眠っていた茶を出してくれました。その場で、何年ものになるのか数え直しました。\n\n「八一年から今まで何年だっけ？」\n「四十二年？」\n「いや、四十五年」\n\n四十五年でした。\n\n「これは、普段飲む普通のプーアル茶とは違います。この酸は、きれいな発酵の酸です」\n\n確かに、きれいな酸味、深い苦味、そしてなんとも言えない熟成香でした。何杯も飲めるものではなく、小さな茶器に一杯を大事にすする。\n\nつくり方も教わりました。プーアル茶には、熟茶と生茶がある。生茶は、摘んで、一度処理をして、圧して塊にして、そのまま置いておく。熟茶は、蓋をして発酵させる。\n\nその説明の時に、こんな言い方が出てきました。\n「麹をつくるのと同じように、温度を上げて、攪拌して、管理していくんです」\n\n麹、という言葉を選んだのは、向こうだった。\n\n昼、台湾新幹線で新竹へ。": "In Tainan, we were served a forty-five-year-old pu-erh. It came from the first batch made at a state-owned tea factory in China in 1981. It had traveled to Japan and spent forty years in storage in Kyushu.\n\nIt had not returned to the place of its birth, but to the island next door.\n\n“Why did I want you to try this tea? Because it was kept so well in Japan. It hasn’t picked up a single unwanted flavor.”\n\nTo visitors from Japan, our host served tea that had slept in Japan for forty years. We worked out its age again, there and then.\n\n“How long has it been since ’81?”\n“Forty-two years?”\n“No, forty-five.”\n\nForty-five years.\n\n“This is different from the ordinary pu-erh you drink every day. This acidity comes from a clean fermentation.”\n\nAnd there it was: a clean acidity, a deep bitterness, and an aged aroma beyond description. This was not a tea to drink cup after cup. We sipped a single small cup, savoring it.\n\nWe learned something about its making, too. There are two kinds of pu-erh: ripe and raw. For raw pu-erh, the leaves are picked, given an initial processing, pressed into a block, and left to age. Ripe pu-erh is covered and fermented.\n\nDuring the explanation, our host put it this way:\n“Just as you do when making koji, you raise the temperature, turn it, and manage the process.”\n\nOur host was the one who chose the word koji.\n\nAt midday, we took the high-speed train to Hsinchu.", "台南で、四十五年もののプーアル茶をいただきました。1981年、中国国営の茶工場でつくられた、最初のひと山。それが日本に渡って、九州で四十年のあいだ保管されていた。": "In Tainan, we were served a forty-five-year-old pu-erh. It came from the first batch made at a state-owned tea factory in China in 1981. It had traveled to Japan and spent forty years in storage in Kyushu.", "生まれた場所には戻らず、隣の島に戻ってきた。": "It had not returned to the place of its birth, but to the island next door.", "「どうしてこのお茶を飲んでほしいと思ったか。日本で、とてもきれいに保管されていたからです。悪い味がまったくついていない」": "“Why did I want you to try this tea? Because it was kept so well in Japan. It hasn’t picked up a single unwanted flavor.”", "日本から来た私たちに、日本で四十年眠っていた茶を出してくれました。その場で、何年ものになるのか数え直しました。": "To visitors from Japan, our host served tea that had slept in Japan for forty years. We worked out its age again, there and then.", "「八一年から今まで何年だっけ？」": "“How long has it been since ’81?”", "「四十二年？」": "“Forty-two years?”", "「いや、四十五年」": "“No, forty-five.”", "四十五年でした。": "Forty-five years.", "「これは、普段飲む普通のプーアル茶とは違います。この酸は、きれいな発酵の酸です」": "“This is different from the ordinary pu-erh you drink every day. This acidity comes from a clean fermentation.”", "確かに、きれいな酸味、深い苦味、そしてなんとも言えない熟成香でした。何杯も飲めるものではなく、小さな茶器に一杯を大事にすする。": "And there it was: a clean acidity, a deep bitterness, and an aged aroma beyond description. This was not a tea to drink cup after cup. We sipped a single small cup, savoring it.", "つくり方も教わりました。プーアル茶には、熟茶と生茶がある。生茶は、摘んで、一度処理をして、圧して塊にして、そのまま置いておく。熟茶は、蓋をして発酵させる。": "We learned something about its making, too. There are two kinds of pu-erh: ripe and raw. For raw pu-erh, the leaves are picked, given an initial processing, pressed into a block, and left to age. Ripe pu-erh is covered and fermented.", "その説明の時に、こんな言い方が出てきました。": "During the explanation, our host put it this way:", "「麹をつくるのと同じように、温度を上げて、攪拌して、管理していくんです」": "“Just as you do when making koji, you raise the temperature, turn it, and manage the process.”", "麹、という言葉を選んだのは、向こうだった。": "Our host was the one who chose the word koji.", "昼、台湾新幹線で新竹へ。": "At midday, we took the high-speed train to Hsinchu.", "木の卓に並ぶ茶碗": "Teacups on a wooden table", "茶碗（8/14 台南）": "A teacup (Tainan, August 14)", "紐は宝物": "The String Is a Treasure", "8/14 午後": "August 14, afternoon", "新竹・北埔": "Beipu, Hsinchu", "北埔（地域表示）": "Beipu (general area)", "三年で薬になり、七年で宝になる、と言われて来たそう。": "Three years makes it medicine; seven, a treasure. So they say.", "新竹から、迎えに来てくれた素さんの車で山へ。北埔。": "Su-san came to pick us up in Hsinchu, and we drove into the mountains. Beipu.", "台湾の新竹、山の中の集落で、酸柑茶（さんかんちゃ）というお茶を見学させてもらい、飲ませていただきました。台湾の中でも、最もdeepなお茶ではないでしょうか。\n\n元々は客家（台湾に広東や福建から渡った古い民族）が正月に神棚へ供えた柑橘を、捨てるに忍びなくてくり抜いて、茶と草を詰めて、蒸して干してを繰り返したお茶。半年かけて、石みたいに硬い黒い塊になります。\n\nくり抜いた皮に、乾いた草と茶を詰める。紐で縛る。蒸して、干す。それを三回。劉先生のお父さんの代までは、九回やっていた。五代目の劉先生が、三回で仕上げる方法を編み出した。伝統は、変化しながら守られていく。そのあと甕に入れて、暗いところで三年。": "In a mountain village in Hsinchu, we were shown how suangan cha is made, and invited to taste it. It may well be the deepest tea in all of Taiwan.\n\nIt began with the Hakka, a people with a long history who came to Taiwan from Guangdong and Fujian. Unable to bear throwing away the citrus fruit offered on their household altars at New Year, they hollowed it out, filled it with tea and herbs, and repeatedly steamed and dried it. Over six months, it becomes a black lump as hard as stone.\n\nFill the hollowed peel with dried herbs and tea. Tie it with string. Steam it, then dry it. Three times. Until Liu’s father’s generation, they did this nine times. Liu, the fifth-generation maker, devised a way to finish it in three. Tradition is preserved through change. Then it goes into a jar, in the dark, for three years.", "台湾の新竹、山の中の集落で、酸柑茶（さんかんちゃ）というお茶を見学させてもらい、飲ませていただきました。台湾の中でも、最もdeepなお茶ではないでしょうか。": "In a mountain village in Hsinchu, we were shown how suangan cha is made, and invited to taste it. It may well be the deepest tea in all of Taiwan.", "元々は客家（台湾に広東や福建から渡った古い民族）が正月に神棚へ供えた柑橘を、捨てるに忍びなくてくり抜いて、茶と草を詰めて、蒸して干してを繰り返したお茶。半年かけて、石みたいに硬い黒い塊になります。": "It began with the Hakka, a people with a long history who came to Taiwan from Guangdong and Fujian. Unable to bear throwing away the citrus fruit offered on their household altars at New Year, they hollowed it out, filled it with tea and herbs, and repeatedly steamed and dried it. Over six months, it becomes a black lump as hard as stone.", "くり抜いた皮に、乾いた草と茶を詰める。紐で縛る。蒸して、干す。それを三回。劉先生のお父さんの代までは、九回やっていた。五代目の劉先生が、三回で仕上げる方法を編み出した。伝統は、変化しながら守られていく。そのあと甕に入れて、暗いところで三年。": "Fill the hollowed peel with dried herbs and tea. Tie it with string. Steam it, then dry it. Three times. Until Liu’s father’s generation, they did this nine times. Liu, the fifth-generation maker, devised a way to finish it in three. Tradition is preserved through change. Then it goes into a jar, in the dark, for three years.", "「半年」は投稿の言葉。": "“Six months” is the wording from our social media post.", "干し場いっぱいに並ぶ酸柑茶": "Suangan cha laid out across the drying area", "酸柑茶の干し場（8/14 17:41 北埔）": "The drying area for suangan cha (Beipu, August 14, 17:41)", "手に持った酸柑茶": "Suangan cha, held in a hand", "酸柑茶（8/14 北埔）": "Suangan cha (Beipu, August 14)", "甕の布を縛る手": "Hands tying the cloth over a jar", "柑橘に詰める手（8/14 18:12）": "Hands filling a hollowed-out citrus fruit (August 14, 18:12)", "酸柑茶を寝かせる甕": "Jars where suangan cha is left to age", "甕（8/14）": "Earthenware jars (August 14)", "酸柑茶の工房": "The suangan cha workshop", "北埔・作業場（8/14）": "The workshop, Beipu (August 14)", "北埔": "Beipu", "「客家の人は、茶が美味しいかどうかを気にしない。効くかどうかが問題なんです」": "“Hakka people don’t worry about whether the tea tastes good. What matters is whether it works.”", "作っているのは劉還月先生（りゅう先生）という民俗学者の方で、台湾中の消えかけた暮らしを歩いて記録してきた人でした。": "The maker, Liu Huan-yue, is a folklorist who has walked all over Taiwan documenting ways of life on the verge of disappearing.", "百冊の本を書いた、と先生は言う。六十歳を過ぎてから酸柑茶の五代目を正式に継いで、茶に専念されている、とも話してくれた。\n\nその言葉が、ずっと残っています。たくさんの種類を飲ませてもらいましたが、滋味深い薬草茶という趣で、確かに飲むほどに体と頭が整う印象がありました。20個、日本へのお土産に買いました。": "He says he has written a hundred books. He also told us that, after turning sixty, he formally took over as the fifth generation to make suangan cha and devoted himself to the tea.\n\nThose words have stayed with me. We tasted many varieties. They had the deep, nourishing flavors of herbal tea, and I did feel, as I drank, that my body and mind were settling into balance. We bought twenty to take home to Japan.", "百冊の本を書いた、と先生は言う。六十歳を過ぎてから酸柑茶の五代目を正式に継いで、茶に専念されている、とも話してくれた。": "He says he has written a hundred books. He also told us that, after turning sixty, he formally took over as the fifth generation to make suangan cha and devoted himself to the tea.", "その言葉が、ずっと残っています。たくさんの種類を飲ませてもらいましたが、滋味深い薬草茶という趣で、確かに飲むほどに体と頭が整う印象がありました。20個、日本へのお土産に買いました。": "Those words have stayed with me. We tasted many varieties. They had the deep, nourishing flavors of herbal tea, and I did feel, as I drank, that my body and mind were settling into balance. We bought twenty to take home to Japan.", "「五代目」は外部資料で確認できていない。": "We have not been able to verify “fifth generation” in outside sources.", "作業場で話す劉先生": "Liu speaking in the workshop", "劉先生（8/14 18:33）": "Liu Huan-yue (August 14, 18:33)", "「これは宝物だから」": "“Because this is a treasure.”", "その話を聞いたのは、旅の六日目だった。": "We heard that story on the sixth day of our journey.", "新竹の山の中、北埔。\n酸柑茶をつくる劉還月先生の作業場に、古い紐の束が残されていた。\n\n柑橘の皮に茶と草を詰めて、縛って、蒸して、干す。\n縛るための紐だ。何度も使われて、黒ずんでいる。\n\nいまは新しい紐で縛っている、と先生は言った。\nそれでも、古い紐は捨てない。": "Beipu, in the mountains of Hsinchu.\nIn the workshop of Liu Huan-yue, who makes suangan cha, a bundle of old string had been kept.\n\nFill a citrus peel with tea and herbs. Tie it up, steam it, dry it.\nThis was the string used to tie it. Used again and again, it had turned dark.\n\nHe told us he uses new string now.\nBut he still keeps the old string.", "新竹の山の中、北埔。": "Beipu, in the mountains of Hsinchu.", "酸柑茶をつくる劉還月先生の作業場に、古い紐の束が残されていた。": "In the workshop of Liu Huan-yue, who makes suangan cha, a bundle of old string had been kept.", "柑橘の皮に茶と草を詰めて、縛って、蒸して、干す。": "Fill a citrus peel with tea and herbs. Tie it up, steam it, dry it.", "縛るための紐だ。何度も使われて、黒ずんでいる。": "This was the string used to tie it. Used again and again, it had turned dark.", "いまは新しい紐で縛っている、と先生は言った。": "He told us he uses new string now.", "それでも、古い紐は捨てない。": "But he still keeps the old string.", "紐で縛られた酸柑茶": "Suangan cha tied with string", "古い紐の束（8/14 北埔）": "A bundle of old string (Beipu, August 14)", "だから、うまくいっていた時の紐を、捨てない。": "That is why he keeps the string from the batches that went well.", "いまは発酵の菌環境が良いから新しい紐で作っている。でも将来、味が良くならないときは、これまで使ってきた紐のストックを使う。そこに有用な微生物がまだ生きていると考えている、と。\n\nこの紐は宝貝（宝物）だ、と。\n\n先生が台北から北埔に戻ってきたころ、三年ほどは、うまくいくのが半分以下だったそうです。環境をつくるのに時間がかかった。最初は、蒸したら菌が死んでしまうと思っていた。でも違った。温度と湿度が合えば、菌は自分で付く。\n\nこれは高知の碁石茶が特定の筵（◯◯さんの家のが良いとか言いながら）を繰り返し使うのと、日本酒の蔵に蔵付き酵母がいるのと、同じ話だと思いました。\n菌は道具と場所に棲んでいる。だから仕込む前に、場を整える。\n\nわたしたちが「発酵」と呼んでいるものと、りゅう先生の言う「發酵」は、たぶん少し違うものを指しています。ふつうに考えれば、蒸すことも干すことも殺菌の工程です。それでも作り手は、発酵と呼ぶ。\n\nここにも、まだ、誰も辿り着いていない発酵の世界がありそうです。": "The microbial environment is good for fermentation now, he told us, so he uses new string. But if the flavor ever stops developing well, he will use his stock of old string. He believes beneficial microorganisms are still living in it.\n\nThis string is baobei—a treasure, he said.\n\nWhen he first returned from Taipei to Beipu, fewer than half his batches succeeded, for about three years. It took time to establish the right environment. At first, he had thought steaming would kill the microbes. But he had been wrong. With the right temperature and humidity, the microbes would colonize the tea on their own.\n\nI thought of the makers of Goishicha, a fermented tea from Kochi, using the same particular straw mats again and again—“The ones from so-and-so’s house are good”—and of the resident yeasts in sake breweries.\nMicrobes live in tools and places. So before making a batch, you prepare the place.\n\nWhat we mean by “fermentation” and what Liu means by fajiao may be slightly different. Ordinarily, we think of steaming and drying as steps that kill microbes. Yet the maker calls this fermentation.\n\nThere may be a world of fermentation here that no one has yet reached.", "いまは発酵の菌環境が良いから新しい紐で作っている。でも将来、味が良くならないときは、これまで使ってきた紐のストックを使う。そこに有用な微生物がまだ生きていると考えている、と。": "The microbial environment is good for fermentation now, he told us, so he uses new string. But if the flavor ever stops developing well, he will use his stock of old string. He believes beneficial microorganisms are still living in it.", "この紐は宝貝（宝物）だ、と。": "This string is baobei—a treasure, he said.", "先生が台北から北埔に戻ってきたころ、三年ほどは、うまくいくのが半分以下だったそうです。環境をつくるのに時間がかかった。最初は、蒸したら菌が死んでしまうと思っていた。でも違った。温度と湿度が合えば、菌は自分で付く。": "When he first returned from Taipei to Beipu, fewer than half his batches succeeded, for about three years. It took time to establish the right environment. At first, he had thought steaming would kill the microbes. But he had been wrong. With the right temperature and humidity, the microbes would colonize the tea on their own.", "これは高知の碁石茶が特定の筵（◯◯さんの家のが良いとか言いながら）を繰り返し使うのと、日本酒の蔵に蔵付き酵母がいるのと、同じ話だと思いました。": "I thought of the makers of Goishicha, a fermented tea from Kochi, using the same particular straw mats again and again—“The ones from so-and-so’s house are good”—and of the resident yeasts in sake breweries.", "菌は道具と場所に棲んでいる。だから仕込む前に、場を整える。": "Microbes live in tools and places. So before making a batch, you prepare the place.", "わたしたちが「発酵」と呼んでいるものと、りゅう先生の言う「發酵」は、たぶん少し違うものを指しています。ふつうに考えれば、蒸すことも干すことも殺菌の工程です。それでも作り手は、発酵と呼ぶ。": "What we mean by “fermentation” and what Liu means by fajiao may be slightly different. Ordinarily, we think of steaming and drying as steps that kill microbes. Yet the maker calls this fermentation.", "ここにも、まだ、誰も辿り着いていない発酵の世界がありそうです。": "There may be a world of fermentation here that no one has yet reached.", "古い紐の束（8/14 17:42）": "A bundle of old string (August 14, 17:42)", "8/14 夜": "August 14, evening", "「継いでくれる人は、何人でも、どこでもよい」": "“It doesn’t matter how many people take it on, or where they are.”", "夜、街へ降りて、客家料理。": "That evening, we went down into town for Hakka food.", "「客家の人が来ないと出さない料理」と言われた皿も出てきた。川海老と平麺の炒め物が、いちばん残っている。\n\n食べながら、聞いてみた。なぜ三回なのか。二回でも四回でもなく。\n先生は、いろいろな数字を試した、と言った。それだけで三年かかった。本に書く。\n\n帰ってから、思ったことがある。納豆は大豆を長く煮ることで、熱に耐える芽胞だけを残し、それが枯草菌の発酵になる。三回蒸すのも、雑菌を殺しながら、発酵に寄与する菌だけを残す手法なのかもしれない。\n\n継ぐ人の話になった。\n\n同じ日の昼、作業場では、こうも言っていた。\n利益があるかどうかで、誰が継ぐか、継がないかが決まる。好きなだけでは、やる気は続かない。それが現実。文化としてだけでは続かない。生活の中に入らないと。\n\nどちらも先生の言葉です。": "One dish was introduced as something “we don’t serve unless Hakka people come.” What I remember most is the river shrimp and the stir-fried flat noodles.\n\nOver dinner, we asked: why three times? Why not two, or four?\nHe said he had tried various numbers. That alone had taken three years. He would write about it in a book.\n\nAfter returning home, I had a thought. In making natto, prolonged cooking of the soybeans leaves heat-resistant spores, which then give rise to Bacillus fermentation. Perhaps steaming three times is also a way to kill unwanted microbes while retaining those that contribute to fermentation.\n\nWe talked about who would carry on the work.\n\nEarlier that day, at the workshop, he had also said this:\nWhether it is profitable determines who will take it on and who will not. Loving it is not enough to keep you going. That is the reality. It cannot survive as culture alone. It has to become part of everyday life.\n\nBoth were his words.", "「客家の人が来ないと出さない料理」と言われた皿も出てきた。川海老と平麺の炒め物が、いちばん残っている。": "One dish was introduced as something “we don’t serve unless Hakka people come.” What I remember most is the river shrimp and the stir-fried flat noodles.", "食べながら、聞いてみた。なぜ三回なのか。二回でも四回でもなく。": "Over dinner, we asked: why three times? Why not two, or four?", "先生は、いろいろな数字を試した、と言った。それだけで三年かかった。本に書く。": "He said he had tried various numbers. That alone had taken three years. He would write about it in a book.", "帰ってから、思ったことがある。納豆は大豆を長く煮ることで、熱に耐える芽胞だけを残し、それが枯草菌の発酵になる。三回蒸すのも、雑菌を殺しながら、発酵に寄与する菌だけを残す手法なのかもしれない。": "After returning home, I had a thought. In making natto, prolonged cooking of the soybeans leaves heat-resistant spores, which then give rise to Bacillus fermentation. Perhaps steaming three times is also a way to kill unwanted microbes while retaining those that contribute to fermentation.", "継ぐ人の話になった。": "We talked about who would carry on the work.", "同じ日の昼、作業場では、こうも言っていた。": "Earlier that day, at the workshop, he had also said this:", "利益があるかどうかで、誰が継ぐか、継がないかが決まる。好きなだけでは、やる気は続かない。それが現実。文化としてだけでは続かない。生活の中に入らないと。": "Whether it is profitable determines who will take it on and who will not. Loving it is not enough to keep you going. That is the reality. It cannot survive as culture alone. It has to become part of everyday life.", "どちらも先生の言葉です。": "Both were his words.", "川海老と平麺の炒め物": "Stir-fried river shrimp and flat noodles", "客家料理の川海老（8/14 20:18）": "River shrimp at a Hakka restaurant (August 14, 20:18)", "獲らない季節": "The Season of Not Taking", "8/15 夜": "August 15, evening", "台北・迪化街": "Dihua Street, Taipei", "迪化街": "Dihua Street", "台湾一周の最後の夜に、今年最後の花火が上がりました。": "On the last night of our journey around Taiwan, the year’s final fireworks went up.", "朝、北埔の古い街を歩いた。寺、客家茶の店、古い町並み。": "In the morning, we walked through old Beipu. Temples, Hakka tea shops, old streets.", "夕方、台北に着いた。\n\n台湾・台北の迪化街（ディーファーストリート）。\n\nその夜、少し遅がけにメーガンさんの「發酵迷」に、山から降りてきた家族が訪ねてきました。\n\n台湾原住民族・泰雅族（タイヤル族）の、集落のリーダーです。": "By evening, we were in Taipei.\n\nDihua Street, Taipei, Taiwan.\n\nLater that evening, a family who had come down from the mountains visited Megan’s Fajiaomi.\n\nThey were the family of an Atayal village leader. The Atayal are one of Taiwan’s Indigenous peoples.", "夕方、台北に着いた。": "By evening, we were in Taipei.", "台湾・台北の迪化街（ディーファーストリート）。": "Dihua Street, Taipei, Taiwan.", "その夜、少し遅がけにメーガンさんの「發酵迷」に、山から降りてきた家族が訪ねてきました。": "Later that evening, a family who had come down from the mountains visited Megan’s Fajiaomi.", "台湾原住民族・泰雅族（タイヤル族）の、集落のリーダーです。": "They were the family of an Atayal village leader. The Atayal are one of Taiwan’s Indigenous peoples.", "リーダーのお名前と集落名は書かない。": "We do not give the leader’s name or the name of his village.", "迪化街の夜空に上がる花火": "Fireworks in the night sky over Dihua Street", "花火（迪化街）": "Fireworks (Dihua Street)", "台北・發酵迷": "Fajiaomi, Taipei", "言葉の前に、親近感あり。": "We felt close before we had words.", "出会うなり、歓迎の踊りを見せてくれました。": "As soon as we met, he showed us a dance of welcome.", "そしてなぜか知っていた日本の餅つきの「返し」の動きもコミカルに真似してくれました。\n\n踊って、真似して、みんなで爆笑。": "Then, somehow knowing the movements, he did a comical imitation of the person turning the rice between blows in Japanese mochi-pounding.\n\nDancing, mimicking, everyone roaring with laughter.", "そしてなぜか知っていた日本の餅つきの「返し」の動きもコミカルに真似してくれました。": "Then, somehow knowing the movements, he did a comical imitation of the person turning the rice between blows in Japanese mochi-pounding.", "踊って、真似して、みんなで爆笑。": "Dancing, mimicking, everyone roaring with laughter.", "發酵迷に集まった人たちの集合写真": "A group photo of the people gathered at Fajiaomi", "リーダー（8/15 21:34）": "The leader (August 15, 21:34)", "歓迎の踊り（動画の静止画）": "The welcome dance (still from a video)", "踊り（8/15・動画より）": "The dance (August 15, from a video)", "餅つきの返しを真似する人（動画の静止画）": "Someone imitating the turning of the rice in mochi-pounding (still from a video)", "餅つきの「返し」（8/15・動画より）": "The mochi-pounding “turn” (August 15, from a video)", "「中くらいがいちばんいい」": "“Medium-sized ones are best”", "そして気づけば、魚の発酵の話。": "Before we knew it, we were talking about fermenting fish.", "そのあと、縄文の話になりました。\n\n日本にも、土地にずっと暮らしてきた人たちがいて、私たちの中にもその記憶が残っているのかもしれない。\n\nそんな話をすると、リーダーも頷いていました。\n\nまず塩をまぶす。\nそれから台所へ運ぶ。\n\n大きい魚は身が硬いから、漬かるまでに時間がかかる。\n小さいのは川へ返す。\n「中くらいがいちばんいい」と、手で大きさをつくって教えてくれました。": "Then we began talking about Japan’s Jomon people.\n\nJapan, too, had people who had lived with the land for a very long time. Perhaps some memory of them remains in us.\n\nAs we talked about that, the leader nodded.\n\nFirst, coat the fish with salt.\nThen take it to the kitchen.\n\nLarge fish have firm flesh, so they take longer to cure.\nThe small ones go back into the river.\n“Medium-sized ones are best,” he said, showing us the size with his hands.", "そのあと、縄文の話になりました。": "Then we began talking about Japan’s Jomon people.", "日本にも、土地にずっと暮らしてきた人たちがいて、私たちの中にもその記憶が残っているのかもしれない。": "Japan, too, had people who had lived with the land for a very long time. Perhaps some memory of them remains in us.", "そんな話をすると、リーダーも頷いていました。": "As we talked about that, the leader nodded.", "まず塩をまぶす。": "First, coat the fish with salt.", "それから台所へ運ぶ。": "Then take it to the kitchen.", "大きい魚は身が硬いから、漬かるまでに時間がかかる。": "Large fish have firm flesh, so they take longer to cure.", "小さいのは川へ返す。": "The small ones go back into the river.", "「中くらいがいちばんいい」と、手で大きさをつくって教えてくれました。": "“Medium-sized ones are best,” he said, showing us the size with his hands.", "「私たちは、自然を母だと思っています」": "“We think of nature as our mother.”", "途中で、獲物を獲らない時期の話になりました。": "At some point, we began talking about the season when they do not catch anything.", "タイヤル族には、風習として、春の三月から五月は魚も獣も獲らない。\nどの動物も子どもを産み、育てる時期だから、と。\n\nだから猟に出るのはそれ以外。\n特に山の猟は十二月だけとか。\n\nそれは誰かに禁じられているのではなく、自分たちで決めていることなんだそうです。\n\n日本にも「母なる湖」と呼ぶ土地がありますよ、と返したり。\n\n帰り際に、こんなことも言われました。\n\n「日本人と泰雅族は、暮らしのかたちが近い。自分のことは自分でやる。人に迷惑をかけたがらないところが、よく似ている」\n\n花火を見に降りてきた、その帰りの立ち話でした。": "Among the Atayal, he said, it is customary not to take fish or animals from March to May, in spring.\nThat is when the animals are giving birth and raising their young.\n\nSo hunting takes place outside those months.\nHunting in the mountains, he said, was limited to December.\n\nThis was not a prohibition imposed on them. It was something they had decided for themselves.\n\nWe replied that there are places in Japan where people speak of a “mother lake,” too.\n\nAs we were leaving, he also said this:\n\n“The Japanese and the Atayal have similar ways of living. We do things for ourselves. We don’t like troubling other people. In that way, we’re very much alike.”\n\nIt was just a conversation on their way home, after coming down to see the fireworks.", "タイヤル族には、風習として、春の三月から五月は魚も獣も獲らない。": "Among the Atayal, he said, it is customary not to take fish or animals from March to May, in spring.", "どの動物も子どもを産み、育てる時期だから、と。": "That is when the animals are giving birth and raising their young.", "だから猟に出るのはそれ以外。": "So hunting takes place outside those months.", "特に山の猟は十二月だけとか。": "Hunting in the mountains, he said, was limited to December.", "それは誰かに禁じられているのではなく、自分たちで決めていることなんだそうです。": "This was not a prohibition imposed on them. It was something they had decided for themselves.", "日本にも「母なる湖」と呼ぶ土地がありますよ、と返したり。": "We replied that there are places in Japan where people speak of a “mother lake,” too.", "帰り際に、こんなことも言われました。": "As we were leaving, he also said this:", "「日本人と泰雅族は、暮らしのかたちが近い。自分のことは自分でやる。人に迷惑をかけたがらないところが、よく似ている」": "“The Japanese and the Atayal have similar ways of living. We do things for ourselves. We don’t like troubling other people. In that way, we’re very much alike.”", "花火を見に降りてきた、その帰りの立ち話でした。": "It was just a conversation on their way home, after coming down to see the fireworks.", "月はリーダーの言葉のまま。": "The months are those the leader gave us.", "赤い提灯の下がった通路": "A passage hung with red lanterns", "赤い提灯の通路（8/15 22:00）": "The passage of red lanterns (August 15, 22:00)", "帰国後": "After returning home", "京都": "Kyoto", "あの晩、順番がひとつ入れ替わりました。": "That evening, something changed order in my mind.", "京都に帰ってきてから、まだ考えています。": "Back in Kyoto, I am still thinking about it.", "保存したいから発酵させてきた、と思っていました。\nそうではなくて、獲らない時期があるから、発酵が必然になる。\n自然のほうに休みがあって、人間がその休みに合わせた。\n\nリーダーがそう言ったのではない。リーダーは、獲らない時期のことと、魚の漬け方を、教えてくれただけ。": "I had thought people fermented food because they wanted to preserve it.\nBut perhaps it was the season of not taking that made fermentation necessary.\nNature had a time of rest, and people arranged their lives around it.\n\nThe leader did not say that. He simply told us about the season when they do not catch anything, and how they cure fish.", "保存したいから発酵させてきた、と思っていました。": "I had thought people fermented food because they wanted to preserve it.", "そうではなくて、獲らない時期があるから、発酵が必然になる。": "But perhaps it was the season of not taking that made fermentation necessary.", "自然のほうに休みがあって、人間がその休みに合わせた。": "Nature had a time of rest, and people arranged their lives around it.", "リーダーがそう言ったのではない。リーダーは、獲らない時期のことと、魚の漬け方を、教えてくれただけ。": "The leader did not say that. He simply told us about the season when they do not catch anything, and how they cure fish.", "ぬか床を渡す": "Passing On the Nukadoko", "台北・大稻埕": "Dadaocheng, Taipei", "大稻埕": "Dadaocheng", "手作りを手放さないまま、どこまで大きくできるのか。": "How much can we grow while keeping things handmade?", "台北・大稻埕の古い建物で講演をしました。": "We gave a talk in an old building in Dadaocheng, Taipei.", "台湾の発酵ブランド「發酵迷」のメーガンさんが、開いてくれた会です。\n\n赤い提灯の下がった通路を抜けて、奥へ入っていく。\n\n命は命で元気になる。\n発酵食を台所に取り戻す♪\n\nからスタートしました。\n\n話したのは、三つです。\n\nひとつ、ぬか床のこと。\n台湾でも、始める人が増えてきました。\n\nふたつ、カモシカがどうして始まったのか。\n十二年前に何があって、この店ができたのか。\n\nみっつ、うまくいっていないこと。\n三つ目を、いちばん長く話しました。\n\n守りたいものと、売れるもののあいだで、どう引き裂かれてきたか。\n\n手作りを手放さないまま、どこまで大きくできるのか。うまくいった話は、あまりしませんでした。": "Megan, of the Taiwanese fermentation brand Fajiaomi, had organized it for us.\n\nWe walked beneath the red lanterns, through the passage, and into the rooms beyond.\n\nLife nourishes life.\nBringing fermented food back to the kitchen♪\n\nThose were our opening words.\n\nWe talked about three things.\n\nFirst, nukadoko.\nMore and more people in Taiwan are starting their own.\n\nSecond, how Kamoshika began.\nWhat happened twelve years ago that brought this restaurant into being.\n\nThird, the things that are not going well.\nWe spent the most time on the third.\n\nHow we have been pulled between what we want to preserve and what will sell.\n\nHow much can we grow while keeping things handmade? We did not spend much time on success stories.", "台湾の発酵ブランド「發酵迷」のメーガンさんが、開いてくれた会です。": "Megan, of the Taiwanese fermentation brand Fajiaomi, had organized it for us.", "赤い提灯の下がった通路を抜けて、奥へ入っていく。": "We walked beneath the red lanterns, through the passage, and into the rooms beyond.", "命は命で元気になる。": "Life nourishes life.", "発酵食を台所に取り戻す♪": "Bringing fermented food back to the kitchen♪", "からスタートしました。": "Those were our opening words.", "話したのは、三つです。": "We talked about three things.", "ひとつ、ぬか床のこと。": "First, nukadoko.", "台湾でも、始める人が増えてきました。": "More and more people in Taiwan are starting their own.", "ふたつ、カモシカがどうして始まったのか。": "Second, how Kamoshika began.", "十二年前に何があって、この店ができたのか。": "What happened twelve years ago that brought this restaurant into being.", "みっつ、うまくいっていないこと。": "Third, the things that are not going well.", "三つ目を、いちばん長く話しました。": "We spent the most time on the third.", "守りたいものと、売れるもののあいだで、どう引き裂かれてきたか。": "How we have been pulled between what we want to preserve and what will sell.", "手作りを手放さないまま、どこまで大きくできるのか。うまくいった話は、あまりしませんでした。": "How much can we grow while keeping things handmade? We did not spend much time on success stories.", "三つ目を長く話したのは、会場で手を挙げてもらって一番多かったから。": "We spent the most time on the third topic because it received the most votes when we asked for a show of hands.", "赤い提灯の通路": "The passage of red lanterns", "講演会場で手を挙げる人たち": "People raising their hands at the talk", "会場・挙手（8/16 12:48）": "The audience, hands raised (August 16, 12:48)", "「ぬか床をもうやっている人、手を挙げてください」": "“Please raise your hand if you already keep a nukadoko.”", "会場の半分以上の方はすでにはじめている人たちでした。数年前は考えられない風景。": "More than half the people there had already started. A sight we could not have imagined a few years ago.", "質問が出た。手づくりから量産へ、そのあいだに何があったのか。\n\n恵の答えは、こうだった。\nまず一キロのぬか床をつくって、箱に入れて売るところから始めた。「重い」と言われて、袋に変えた。最初は自分で、相手の手に直接渡していた。そのうち、人に売ってもらうようになる。量が増える。手ではもう追いつかなくなる。そこで、手を機械の手に変えていく。量が増えるのに合わせて、小さな機械を少しずつ入れていった。\n\n台所の質問も出た。毎日混ぜるというのは、毎日野菜を入れるということか。\n野菜が入っていなければ、毎日混ぜなくていい。入っているなら、食べない日でもひと混ぜ。\nそして、いま台湾に来ているこの数日、京都の床を混ぜてくれる人はいない。そういうときは、上に塩を置いておく。それだけ。帰るころには少し酸っぱくなっている。でもそれは対処すればいい。\n\n出張ぬか床クリニックも開くと、みなさんが身を乗り出してきました。\n\n台所の悩みは、日本で聞くものとほとんど同じでした。海を越えても、そんなに大きくは変わらない。": "Someone asked about the move from making things by hand to producing in quantity. What had happened along the way?\n\nMegumi answered like this.\nWe began by making one-kilogram portions of nukadoko, packing them in boxes, and selling them. People said they were heavy, so we switched to bags. At first, we handed each one directly to the person buying it. In time, other people began selling it for us. Quantities grew. Hands alone could no longer keep up. So the work of those hands gradually passed to machines. As quantities increased, we introduced small machines, a little at a time.\n\nThere were questions from the kitchen, too. Does mixing it every day mean adding vegetables every day?\nIf there are no vegetables in the bed, it does not need daily mixing. If there are, give it a mix even on days when you will not eat any.\nAnd during these few days in Taiwan, there was no one at home in Kyoto to mix the bed. At times like that, put salt on top. That is all. It will be a little sour by the time you return, but you can deal with that.\n\nWhen we opened our traveling nukadoko clinic, everyone leaned forward.\n\nTheir kitchen worries were almost the same as the ones we hear in Japan. Across the sea, they did not change very much.", "質問が出た。手づくりから量産へ、そのあいだに何があったのか。": "Someone asked about the move from making things by hand to producing in quantity. What had happened along the way?", "恵の答えは、こうだった。": "Megumi answered like this.", "まず一キロのぬか床をつくって、箱に入れて売るところから始めた。「重い」と言われて、袋に変えた。最初は自分で、相手の手に直接渡していた。そのうち、人に売ってもらうようになる。量が増える。手ではもう追いつかなくなる。そこで、手を機械の手に変えていく。量が増えるのに合わせて、小さな機械を少しずつ入れていった。": "We began by making one-kilogram portions of nukadoko, packing them in boxes, and selling them. People said they were heavy, so we switched to bags. At first, we handed each one directly to the person buying it. In time, other people began selling it for us. Quantities grew. Hands alone could no longer keep up. So the work of those hands gradually passed to machines. As quantities increased, we introduced small machines, a little at a time.", "台所の質問も出た。毎日混ぜるというのは、毎日野菜を入れるということか。": "There were questions from the kitchen, too. Does mixing it every day mean adding vegetables every day?", "野菜が入っていなければ、毎日混ぜなくていい。入っているなら、食べない日でもひと混ぜ。": "If there are no vegetables in the bed, it does not need daily mixing. If there are, give it a mix even on days when you will not eat any.", "そして、いま台湾に来ているこの数日、京都の床を混ぜてくれる人はいない。そういうときは、上に塩を置いておく。それだけ。帰るころには少し酸っぱくなっている。でもそれは対処すればいい。": "And during these few days in Taiwan, there was no one at home in Kyoto to mix the bed. At times like that, put salt on top. That is all. It will be a little sour by the time you return, but you can deal with that.", "出張ぬか床クリニックも開くと、みなさんが身を乗り出してきました。": "When we opened our traveling nukadoko clinic, everyone leaned forward.", "台所の悩みは、日本で聞くものとほとんど同じでした。海を越えても、そんなに大きくは変わらない。": "Their kitchen worries were almost the same as the ones we hear in Japan. Across the sea, they did not change very much.", "恵が日本語で答え、メーガンさんが通訳。": "Megumi answered in Japanese, and Megan interpreted.", "「一人ひとりが、消費者であるだけでなく、生産者にもなっていくこと」": "“For each of us to become a producer, as well as a consumer.”", "京都から抱えてきた熟成ぬか床を、分けて渡した。": "We divided up the mature nukadoko we had carried from Kyoto and passed it around.", "前の晩の原住民のリーダーが、また顔を出してくれた。\n\n最後に、メーガンさんが会場に問いを投げました。\n\n発酵は、みなさんの命を癒してきた。\nそれは今、あなたに何をもたらしていますか。\nあなたは発酵と一緒に、何を創り出したいですか。": "The Indigenous leader we had met the night before came by again.\n\nAt the end, Megan put a question to the room.\n\nFermentation has brought healing to your lives.\nWhat is it bringing you now?\nWhat would you like to create together with fermentation?", "前の晩の原住民のリーダーが、また顔を出してくれた。": "The Indigenous leader we had met the night before came by again.", "最後に、メーガンさんが会場に問いを投げました。": "At the end, Megan put a question to the room.", "発酵は、みなさんの命を癒してきた。": "Fermentation has brought healing to your lives.", "それは今、あなたに何をもたらしていますか。": "What is it bringing you now?", "あなたは発酵と一緒に、何を創り出したいですか。": "What would you like to create together with fermentation?", "ぬか床を手渡す恵": "Megumi handing over nukadoko", "ぬか床の手渡し（8/16 12:42）": "Handing over the nukadoko (August 16, 12:42)", "ぬか床を受け取る人と恵": "Megumi with someone receiving nukadoko", "ぬか床の手渡し（8/16）": "Handing over the nukadoko (August 16)", "8/16 午後": "August 16, afternoon", "呷二嘴": "Jia Nung Tsui", "台湾一周、終了。": "Our journey around Taiwan was over.", "終わってから、呷二嘴（ジャッ・ヌン・ツイ）の米苔目（ビータイバッ）のかき氷と、遅めのお昼に鮫料理の屋台で、エビ入りの水餃子などなど。": "Afterward, shaved ice with <i>bi tai bak</i> rice noodles at Jia Nung Tsui (呷二嘴). Then a late lunch at a street stall serving shark dishes: boiled shrimp dumplings, among other things.", "米苔目のかき氷": "Shaved ice with bi tai bak rice noodles", "かき氷（8/16 13:57）": "Shaved ice (August 16, 13:57)", "南へ南へ運ばれていった": "Carried Further South", "関西空港": "Kansai Airport", "台北": "Taipei", "今度は、こちらが行く番だった。": "Now it was our turn to visit.", "台北に「發酵迷」という発酵のブランドがある。メーガンさんが率いている。": "In Taipei, there is a fermentation brand called Fajiaomi (發酵迷). It is led by Megan.", "彼女が京都に来たとき、私たちは京都の発酵を案内した。\nグッドネイチャーステーションの「発酵する台湾」では、彼女を招いて話してもらった。\n\n2026年8月9日から16日。七泊八日で、台湾をぐるりと一周する。\n京都・嵐山の発酵食堂が、熟成ぬか床をひとつ抱えて行く。\n島一周分の人を、メーガンさんがつないでくれた。": "When she came to Kyoto, we showed her around its world of fermentation.\nWe also invited her to speak at “Fermenting Taiwan,” an event at GOOD NATURE STATION.\n\nAugust 9–16, 2026. Eight days and seven nights, all the way around Taiwan.\nWe were setting out from our fermentation restaurant in Arashiyama, Kyoto, carrying a mature nukadoko.\nMegan had connected us with people all around the island.", "彼女が京都に来たとき、私たちは京都の発酵を案内した。": "When she came to Kyoto, we showed her around its world of fermentation.", "グッドネイチャーステーションの「発酵する台湾」では、彼女を招いて話してもらった。": "We also invited her to speak at “Fermenting Taiwan,” an event at GOOD NATURE STATION.", "2026年8月9日から16日。七泊八日で、台湾をぐるりと一周する。": "August 9–16, 2026. Eight days and seven nights, all the way around Taiwan.", "京都・嵐山の発酵食堂が、熟成ぬか床をひとつ抱えて行く。": "We were setting out from our fermentation restaurant in Arashiyama, Kyoto, carrying a mature nukadoko.", "島一周分の人を、メーガンさんがつないでくれた。": "Megan had connected us with people all around the island.", "8/9 台北 ─ 8/10 東海岸を南へ ─ 8/11 花蓮・富里 ─ 8/12 台東 ─ 8/13 屏東・台南 ─ 8/14 台南・新竹北埔 ─ 8/15 台北 ─ 8/16 台北": "August 9 Taipei — August 10 South along the east coast — August 11 Hualien and Fuli — August 12 Taitung — August 13 Pingtung and Tainan — August 14 Tainan and Beipu, Hsinchu — August 15 Taipei — August 16 Taipei", "店の前に立つメーガンさん": "Megan standing in front of a shop", "メーガンさん（8/10 朝陽漁港）": "Megan (Chaoyang Fishing Harbor, August 10)", "関西空港 → 台北": "Kansai Airport → Taipei", "台風の名前は「白海豚」だった。": "The typhoon was called “White Dolphin.”", "飛んだ。": "We took off.", "その日、台湾では国内線79便、国際線115便が欠航した。\n関西空港15時30分発のピーチは、ぎりぎりまで飛ぶかどうかわからなかった。前の便は飛んでいない。\n\n飛んだ。\n17時30分、桃園。": "That day, 79 domestic flights and 115 international flights were cancelled in Taiwan.\nUntil the last moment, we did not know whether our 15:30 Peach flight from Kansai Airport would leave. The flight before ours had not.\n\nWe took off.\n17:30. Taoyuan.", "その日、台湾では国内線79便、国際線115便が欠航した。": "That day, 79 domestic flights and 115 international flights were cancelled in Taiwan.", "関西空港15時30分発のピーチは、ぎりぎりまで飛ぶかどうかわからなかった。前の便は飛んでいない。": "Until the last moment, we did not know whether our 15:30 Peach flight from Kansai Airport would leave. The flight before ours had not.", "17時30分、桃園。": "17:30. Taoyuan.", "欠航数は中央社（CNA）2026年8月9日の記事による。": "The cancellation figures come from a Central News Agency (CNA) article dated August 9, 2026.", "ピーチ航空の飛行機へ搭乗する人": "People boarding a Peach Aviation plane", "搭乗橋（8/9 15:32 関西空港）": "The boarding bridge (Kansai Airport, August 9, 15:32)", "8/9 夜": "August 9, evening", "台北・内湖": "Neihu, Taipei", "梅乃厨": "梅乃廚", "久しぶりな景色、仲間。": "Sights and friends we had not seen for a while.", "台湾一周の最初の夜は、それだけ。": "That was our first night on the journey around Taiwan. Just that.", "夜、内湖のおでん屋「梅乃厨」で、メーガンさんとビビアンさんが待っていた。赤い提灯。\n\n久しぶりな景色、仲間。ここからどんな人や発酵にであえるのだろう。\n\n一週間後の8月16日には、台北で「京都的発酵生活」という小さな講座をすることになっていた。ぬか床をはじめているメーガンさんの仲間たちが30名以上、もう申し込んで、満席だという。": "That evening, Megan and Vivian were waiting at the oden restaurant 梅乃廚 in Neihu. Red lanterns.\n\nSights and friends we had not seen for a while. Who would we meet from here? What kinds of fermentation would we find?\n\nA week later, on August 16, we were to give a small workshop in Taipei called “Fermentation in Everyday Kyoto Life.” More than thirty people from Megan’s circle, who had begun keeping nukadoko, had already signed up. We were told it was fully booked.", "夜、内湖のおでん屋「梅乃厨」で、メーガンさんとビビアンさんが待っていた。赤い提灯。": "That evening, Megan and Vivian were waiting at the oden restaurant 梅乃廚 in Neihu. Red lanterns.", "久しぶりな景色、仲間。ここからどんな人や発酵にであえるのだろう。": "Sights and friends we had not seen for a while. Who would we meet from here? What kinds of fermentation would we find?", "一週間後の8月16日には、台北で「京都的発酵生活」という小さな講座をすることになっていた。ぬか床をはじめているメーガンさんの仲間たちが30名以上、もう申し込んで、満席だという。": "A week later, on August 16, we were to give a small workshop in Taipei called “Fermentation in Everyday Kyoto Life.” More than thirty people from Megan’s circle, who had begun keeping nukadoko, had already signed up. We were told it was fully booked.", "赤い提灯の下で食事をする人たち": "People eating beneath red lanterns", "梅乃厨の赤提灯（8/9 21:24）": "Red lanterns at 梅乃廚 (August 9, 21:24)", "台北の店内で並ぶ二人": "Two people side by side in a restaurant in Taipei", "台北（8/9 夜）": "Taipei (August 9, evening)", "食卓を囲む人たち": "People gathered around a table", "夜の食卓": "The evening table", "東海岸を南へ": "South along the east coast", "東海岸（非公開）": "East coast (location withheld)", "進むほど、人が増えていく。": "The further we went, the more people joined us.", "翌朝、メーガンさんとイーゴさんに合流した。": "The next morning, we met up with Megan and Eagle.", "川辺で手作りの朝食を食べて、南へ。\n\n宜蘭から蘇花公路を下る途中で、ドンドンさんファミリーが合流した。海辺でシーフード。エビ、魚、穴子、台湾ビール。\n\n午後、花蓮の南、橋の下で川に入った。\n地元の人たちが、川で勝手にBBQをして、カラオケをして、泳いでいる。その自由さにも癒やされた。": "We ate a homemade breakfast by the river, then headed south.\n\nOn the way down the Suhua Highway from Yilan, Dondon and family joined us. Seafood by the sea. Shrimp, fish, conger eel, Taiwanese beer.\n\nIn the afternoon, south of Hualien, we went into the river beneath a bridge.\nLocal people were having barbecues, singing karaoke, swimming—just as they pleased. That freedom did us good, too.", "川辺で手作りの朝食を食べて、南へ。": "We ate a homemade breakfast by the river, then headed south.", "宜蘭から蘇花公路を下る途中で、ドンドンさんファミリーが合流した。海辺でシーフード。エビ、魚、穴子、台湾ビール。": "On the way down the Suhua Highway from Yilan, Dondon and family joined us. Seafood by the sea. Shrimp, fish, conger eel, Taiwanese beer.", "午後、花蓮の南、橋の下で川に入った。": "In the afternoon, south of Hualien, we went into the river beneath a bridge.", "地元の人たちが、川で勝手にBBQをして、カラオケをして、泳いでいる。その自由さにも癒やされた。": "Local people were having barbecues, singing karaoke, swimming—just as they pleased. That freedom did us good, too.", "橋の下の川で過ごす人たち": "People spending time in the river beneath a bridge", "川（8/10 15:00ごろ）": "The river (August 10, around 15:00)", "川に入り思い思いに過ごす人たち": "People in the river, each enjoying it in their own way", "川（8/10 午後）": "The river (August 10, afternoon)", "8/10 夕方": "August 10, evening", "花蓮・玉里": "Yuli, Hualien", "玉里橋頭臭豆腐": "Yuli Bridgehead Stinky Tofu", "臭い。でも、旨い。": "It stinks. But it tastes so good.", "花蓮県玉里、「玉里橋頭臭豆腐」。老若男女の行列。": "Yuli Bridgehead Stinky Tofu, in Yuli, Hualien County. A queue of people of every age.", "お店に近づくだけで凄まじい匂いですが、食べてみると衝撃の旨さ。今まで食べた臭豆腐の中では確実にナンバーワン。\n\n臭豆腐のあの「臭」は、豆腐そのものではなく「臭滷水（しゅうろすい）」という発酵した漬け汁。この汁の中で多様な微生物たちが働き、豆腐のたんぱく質を旨味へと分解していく。\n\n店を出た後、服から靴、カバンまで全身に香りが染み込んだ。\n翌日の靴まで、臭った。\n\n夕日。\n\nこの日は、一つの目的地を訪ねたというより、人と合流しながら、台湾を南へ運ばれていった一日だった。": "The smell hit us before we even reached the shop. Then we tasted it. Astonishingly good. Easily the best stinky tofu we had ever eaten.\n\nThe “stink” comes from a fermented soaking liquid called chou lu shui, rather than the tofu itself. A whole range of microorganisms works in this liquid, breaking down the tofu’s proteins into umami.\n\nBy the time we left, the smell had worked its way into everything: clothes, shoes, bags.\nOur shoes still smelled the next day.\n\nSunset.\n\nIt had been less a day of visiting a particular destination than a day of being carried south through Taiwan, with people joining us along the way.", "お店に近づくだけで凄まじい匂いですが、食べてみると衝撃の旨さ。今まで食べた臭豆腐の中では確実にナンバーワン。": "The smell hit us before we even reached the shop. Then we tasted it. Astonishingly good. Easily the best stinky tofu we had ever eaten.", "臭豆腐のあの「臭」は、豆腐そのものではなく「臭滷水（しゅうろすい）」という発酵した漬け汁。この汁の中で多様な微生物たちが働き、豆腐のたんぱく質を旨味へと分解していく。": "The “stink” comes from a fermented soaking liquid called chou lu shui, rather than the tofu itself. A whole range of microorganisms works in this liquid, breaking down the tofu’s proteins into umami.", "店を出た後、服から靴、カバンまで全身に香りが染み込んだ。": "By the time we left, the smell had worked its way into everything: clothes, shoes, bags.", "翌日の靴まで、臭った。": "Our shoes still smelled the next day.", "夕日。": "Sunset.", "この日は、一つの目的地を訪ねたというより、人と合流しながら、台湾を南へ運ばれていった一日だった。": "It had been less a day of visiting a particular destination than a day of being carried south through Taiwan, with people joining us along the way.", "臭滷水の説明は本人が現地で理解した内容。": "The explanation of stinky-tofu brine reflects our understanding at the time of the visit.", "臭豆腐店に並ぶ人たち": "People queuing at a stinky tofu shop", "臭豆腐の行列（8/10 18:16 玉里）": "The queue for stinky tofu (Yuli, August 10, 18:16)", "キャベツの漬物をのせた臭豆腐": "Stinky tofu topped with pickled cabbage", "一見、揚げ出し豆腐。（8/10 玉里橋頭臭豆腐）": "At first glance, agedashi tofu. (Yuli Bridgehead Stinky Tofu, August 10)", "玉里橋頭臭豆腐の店構えと並ぶ人たち": "The front of Yuli Bridgehead Stinky Tofu, with people queuing", "玉里橋頭臭豆腐の店構え（8/10）": "The front of Yuli Bridgehead Stinky Tofu (August 10)", "番号札の案内が貼られた店の入口": "A shop entrance with a notice about number tickets", "番号札をとって、呼ばれたら注文（8/10）": "Take a number, and order when it’s called (August 10)", "店先の分別ゴミ箱と洗い場": "Sorted rubbish bins and a washing area in front of the shop", "店先の分別ゴミ箱と洗い場（8/10）": "Sorted rubbish bins and a washing area in front of the shop (August 10)", "番号の表示と値段の貼り紙": "A number display and a price list", "玉里橋頭臭豆腐（8/10）": "Yuli Bridgehead Stinky Tofu (August 10)", "夕景と山並み": "Sunset over the mountains", "夕日（8/10）": "Sunset (August 10)", "異文化と繋がるほど、嵐山が深くなる": "Connecting with Other Cultures, Deepening Our Roots in Arashiyama", "ハイテックとハイタッチが、なぜあんなに自然に両立しているのか。": "How do high technology and the human touch coexist so naturally?", "京都に帰ってきてから、まだ考えている。": "Back in Kyoto, I am still thinking.", "テスラの最新の車に乗りながら、梅干しについて必死に追求する。手作りのカゴを真剣に選ぶ。\nそういう姿に、とても微笑ましい気持ちを受けた。\n\nみんなが、心の赴くままに考えて行動しながら、他者をおもんばかることも同時に大切にしている。だから、居心地が結果としてよい。それが土地の風土としての、心理的安全性にもつながっているような気がする。\n\nそしてやはり、原住民族の人たちはインパクトがあった。\n日本に帰ってから、流域というテーマで地元を見直している。土地に住んできた人たちの足跡や想いが、お寺や集落や水利の作り方に出ているからだ。\n自然に従う、自然を治める、自然と時間をかけて付き合う。それは日本にも脈々としてある。": "Riding in the latest Tesla, yet pursuing every detail of umeboshi. Choosing a handmade basket with such care.\nThere was something about it that made me smile.\n\nPeople seemed to think and act as their hearts led them, while placing equal importance on consideration for others. The result was that you felt at ease. I suspect this also contributes to a shared sense of safety—a feeling that you can be yourself—that is part of the character of the place.\n\nAnd the encounters with Indigenous people have stayed with me.\nSince returning to Japan, I have been looking at our own area again through its river basin. The traces and intentions of the people who lived here are visible in its temples, its settlements, and the ways they managed water.\nFollowing nature, managing it, spending time learning to live with it. That, too, runs deep in Japan.", "テスラの最新の車に乗りながら、梅干しについて必死に追求する。手作りのカゴを真剣に選ぶ。": "Riding in the latest Tesla, yet pursuing every detail of umeboshi. Choosing a handmade basket with such care.", "そういう姿に、とても微笑ましい気持ちを受けた。": "There was something about it that made me smile.", "みんなが、心の赴くままに考えて行動しながら、他者をおもんばかることも同時に大切にしている。だから、居心地が結果としてよい。それが土地の風土としての、心理的安全性にもつながっているような気がする。": "People seemed to think and act as their hearts led them, while placing equal importance on consideration for others. The result was that you felt at ease. I suspect this also contributes to a shared sense of safety—a feeling that you can be yourself—that is part of the character of the place.", "そしてやはり、原住民族の人たちはインパクトがあった。": "And the encounters with Indigenous people have stayed with me.", "日本に帰ってから、流域というテーマで地元を見直している。土地に住んできた人たちの足跡や想いが、お寺や集落や水利の作り方に出ているからだ。": "Since returning to Japan, I have been looking at our own area again through its river basin. The traces and intentions of the people who lived here are visible in its temples, its settlements, and the ways they managed water.", "自然に従う、自然を治める、自然と時間をかけて付き合う。それは日本にも脈々としてある。": "Following nature, managing it, spending time learning to live with it. That, too, runs deep in Japan.", "台北・迪化街（8/15）": "Dihua Street, Taipei (August 15)", "台東果子狸咖啡の袋": "A bag from Guozili Coffee, Taitung", "台東果子狸咖啡（8/17 京都）": "Guozili Coffee (果子狸咖啡), Taitung (Kyoto, August 17)", "必然としての発酵。要望に応える発酵。文化継承の発酵。": "Fermentation born of necessity. Fermentation that answers a need. Fermentation that carries culture forward.", "投稿の最後に、三行を書いた。": "At the end of a social media post, I wrote three lines.", "カモシカがやっているのは、要望に応える発酵と、文化継承の発酵だと思った。\nでも、美味しいものを食べたいという根源的なところでは、それも必然なのかもしれない。まだ決めかねている。": "I thought that Kamoshika worked with the second and third: answering needs and carrying culture forward.\nBut at the most basic level—the wish to eat something delicious—perhaps that, too, is a necessity. I am still not sure.", "カモシカがやっているのは、要望に応える発酵と、文化継承の発酵だと思った。": "I thought that Kamoshika worked with the second and third: answering needs and carrying culture forward.", "でも、美味しいものを食べたいという根源的なところでは、それも必然なのかもしれない。まだ決めかねている。": "But at the most basic level—the wish to eat something delicious—perhaps that, too, is a necessity. I am still not sure.", "発酵食堂カモシカの外観": "The front of our restaurant, Kamoshika Fermentation", "嵐山の店（仮置き）": "Our restaurant in Arashiyama (placeholder)", "旅の拾いもんを少し結ぶ。": "Here, I tie together a few things I picked up along the way.", "沖縄の豆腐よう、ふなずし、秋田のきりたんぽ。初めての味を、三回、日本の味で受け止めていた。\nしみじみ美味しい、ということだった。\n\n「土地にあるものが違うから、部落によって違う」。「小さいのは川へ返す」。\nまず母なる自然界があって、それに委ねていく心根がある。そうなると、作法が自ずと生まれて、守られていくのではないか。\n\n簡さんは黒麹をやめた。台南では、麹をつくるのと同じように温度を上げて攪拌すると言った。劉先生は、温度と湿度が合えば菌は自分で付くと言った。\n三人とも、菌を「入れる」のでなく、場をつくる話をしていた。人ができることはベストを尽くすけれど、最後はお任せしている。\n\n「まだ気に入っていません」。「効くかどうかが問題」。「自分たちで決めていること」。\n基準が外にない。基準は、これまで継承されてきた文化と同時に、自分たちの価値観、美意識にある。\n\n劉先生の「生活の中に入らないと文化は続かない」と、恵の「一人ひとりが生産者に」。\n同じことを言っているようで、少し違う。劉先生は、文化の継承と進化で、マーケットにニーズをつくり、さらなる継承を目指していた。恵は、プロシューマーを増やすことが豊かさを増やすことだと言っている。\n劉先生が、野草をなんでも発酵させてお茶にしていたことが、その意味では響いた。": "Okinawan tofuyo, funazushi, kiritanpo from Akita. Three times, I had understood an unfamiliar flavor through something I knew from Japan.\nEach was delicious in a way that went deep.\n\n“It varies from village to village because what grows there is different.” “The small ones go back into the river.”\nFirst there is nature, our mother, and a willingness to entrust ourselves to her. Perhaps ways of doing things arise from that, and are then kept.\n\nJian stopped using black koji. In Tainan, our host described raising the temperature and turning the tea, just as in koji-making. Liu said that, with the right temperature and humidity, the microbes would arrive on their own.\nAll three were talking about creating an environment, rather than “putting in” microbes. They do everything they can, then leave the rest to what happens there.\n\n“I’m not happy with it yet.” “What matters is whether it works.” “We decided for ourselves.”\nThe measure does not come from outside. It lies in the culture handed down to them, and in their own values and sense of beauty.\n\nLiu’s “Culture has to become part of everyday life to survive,” and Megumi’s “For each of us to become a producer.”\nThey sound alike, but there is a difference. By preserving and evolving a culture, Liu was creating demand that would help it pass to another generation. Megumi was saying that more people becoming both producers and consumers would make life richer.\nIn that light, Liu’s practice of fermenting all sorts of wild plants into tea struck a chord.", "沖縄の豆腐よう、ふなずし、秋田のきりたんぽ。初めての味を、三回、日本の味で受け止めていた。": "Okinawan tofuyo, funazushi, kiritanpo from Akita. Three times, I had understood an unfamiliar flavor through something I knew from Japan.", "しみじみ美味しい、ということだった。": "Each was delicious in a way that went deep.", "「土地にあるものが違うから、部落によって違う」。「小さいのは川へ返す」。": "“It varies from village to village because what grows there is different.” “The small ones go back into the river.”", "まず母なる自然界があって、それに委ねていく心根がある。そうなると、作法が自ずと生まれて、守られていくのではないか。": "First there is nature, our mother, and a willingness to entrust ourselves to her. Perhaps ways of doing things arise from that, and are then kept.", "簡さんは黒麹をやめた。台南では、麹をつくるのと同じように温度を上げて攪拌すると言った。劉先生は、温度と湿度が合えば菌は自分で付くと言った。": "Jian stopped using black koji. In Tainan, our host described raising the temperature and turning the tea, just as in koji-making. Liu said that, with the right temperature and humidity, the microbes would arrive on their own.", "三人とも、菌を「入れる」のでなく、場をつくる話をしていた。人ができることはベストを尽くすけれど、最後はお任せしている。": "All three were talking about creating an environment, rather than “putting in” microbes. They do everything they can, then leave the rest to what happens there.", "「まだ気に入っていません」。「効くかどうかが問題」。「自分たちで決めていること」。": "“I’m not happy with it yet.” “What matters is whether it works.” “We decided for ourselves.”", "基準が外にない。基準は、これまで継承されてきた文化と同時に、自分たちの価値観、美意識にある。": "The measure does not come from outside. It lies in the culture handed down to them, and in their own values and sense of beauty.", "劉先生の「生活の中に入らないと文化は続かない」と、恵の「一人ひとりが生産者に」。": "Liu’s “Culture has to become part of everyday life to survive,” and Megumi’s “For each of us to become a producer.”", "同じことを言っているようで、少し違う。劉先生は、文化の継承と進化で、マーケットにニーズをつくり、さらなる継承を目指していた。恵は、プロシューマーを増やすことが豊かさを増やすことだと言っている。": "They sound alike, but there is a difference. By preserving and evolving a culture, Liu was creating demand that would help it pass to another generation. Megumi was saying that more people becoming both producers and consumers would make life richer.", "劉先生が、野草をなんでも発酵させてお茶にしていたことが、その意味では響いた。": "In that light, Liu’s practice of fermenting all sorts of wild plants into tea struck a chord.", "劉先生（8/14 北埔）": "Liu Huan-yue (Beipu, August 14)", "七本の試験管（8/13 屏東）": "Seven test tubes (Pingtung, August 13)", "京都・嵐山": "Arashiyama, Kyoto", "嵐山": "Arashiyama", "異文化と繋がるほど、嵐山が深くなる。": "The more we connect with other cultures, the deeper our roots in Arashiyama grow.", "文化は、どうすれば生きたまま次の人へ渡るのか。": "How can a culture be passed to the next person while still alive?", "旅に出る前から、そう問うていたわけではない。帰ってきて、八日間を振り返るうちに出てきた問いだ。\n\nいまの答えは、こうだ。\nこんな時代だけど、生身で、人と人が繋がり合うこと。\n\nぬか床を渡していたとき、思った。\nこの人たちとは、またどこかで会うよな、と。\n\n嵐山から渡されたぬか床が、拡がっていく。それと同時に、もうすでにそこで、嵐山とつながっている。来てくれるかもしれない。\n\n海外に広げるために、出ていくのではない。\n異文化と繋がるほど、嵐山が深くなる。\n\nこの冊子では、その場で聞いたことと、私たちが理解したことと、帰ってから考えたことを、分けて書いた。この章は、三つ目だけでできている。": "We had not set out with that question. It emerged after we came home, as we looked back over those eight days.\n\nFor now, my answer is this:\nEven in times like these, people meeting and connecting in person.\n\nAs we handed out the nukadoko, I thought:\nWe’ll see these people again somewhere.\n\nThe nukadoko passed on from Arashiyama spreads. And already, wherever it goes, there is a connection back to Arashiyama. Perhaps they will come to visit.\n\nWe do not go abroad in order to expand overseas.\nThrough these connections, our roots in Arashiyama grow deeper.\n\nIn this booklet, we have distinguished what we heard on the spot, how we understood it, and what we thought about after returning home. This chapter consists entirely of the third.", "旅に出る前から、そう問うていたわけではない。帰ってきて、八日間を振り返るうちに出てきた問いだ。": "We had not set out with that question. It emerged after we came home, as we looked back over those eight days.", "いまの答えは、こうだ。": "For now, my answer is this:", "こんな時代だけど、生身で、人と人が繋がり合うこと。": "Even in times like these, people meeting and connecting in person.", "ぬか床を渡していたとき、思った。": "As we handed out the nukadoko, I thought:", "この人たちとは、またどこかで会うよな、と。": "We’ll see these people again somewhere.", "嵐山から渡されたぬか床が、拡がっていく。それと同時に、もうすでにそこで、嵐山とつながっている。来てくれるかもしれない。": "The nukadoko passed on from Arashiyama spreads. And already, wherever it goes, there is a connection back to Arashiyama. Perhaps they will come to visit.", "海外に広げるために、出ていくのではない。": "We do not go abroad in order to expand overseas.", "この冊子では、その場で聞いたことと、私たちが理解したことと、帰ってから考えたことを、分けて書いた。この章は、三つ目だけでできている。": "In this booklet, we have distinguished what we heard on the spot, how we understood it, and what we thought about after returning home. This chapter consists entirely of the third.", "ぬか床の手渡し（8/16 台北）": "Handing over the nukadoko (Taipei, August 16)", "第2章の旅程地図": "Route map for Chapter 2", "台湾本島の白地図": "Blank map of Taiwan’s main island", "第2章の場面を選ぶ": "Choose a scene from Chapter 2", "写真を選ぶ": "Choose a photo", "第2章 土地にあるもので｜台湾一周・発酵の旅": "Chapter 2: What the Land Provides | A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "発酵食堂カモシカ": "Kamoshika Fermentation", "第1章": "Chapter 1", "/　第2章　/": "/　Chapter 2　/", "第3章": "Chapter 3", "第4章": "Chapter 4", "第5章": "Chapter 5", "第6章": "Chapter 6", "第7章": "Chapter 7", "終章": "Epilogue", "発表モード": "Presentation mode", "結構な早朝、<br>私たちは雲の上にいた。": "Very early that morning,<br>we were above the clouds.", "章をたどる　↓": "Follow the chapter　↓", "この章の旅程　富里 → 羅山村 → 東海岸 → 台東": "Route for this chapter: Fuli → Luoshan Village → East coast → Taitung", "地点：写真GPSまたは施設所在地に基づく。非公開の訪問先は地域表示に留める。": "Locations: based on photo GPS data or the venue’s address. Places we visited that are not public are shown by area only.", "豆腐乳の観察ノートをひらく　↓": "Open the notes on doufuru　↓", "この場面を発表モードで見る　↗": "View this scene in presentation mode　↗", "同じ場面を、<br>もう少し見る。": "A little more<br>of the same scene.", "豆腐乳<br>豆腐が保存食になるまで": "Doufuru<br>How tofu becomes a food that keeps", "旅の本文とは別に、事実と未解明なことを分けて残す。": "Kept apart from the travel text: what is known, and what remains unknown.", "何が発酵しているか": "What is fermenting?", "大豆から作った豆腐。塩漬けにし、米麹や酒と一緒に瓶で熟成させる。今回の現地例は、泥火山の水で固めた豆腐から始まる。": "Tofu made from soybeans. It is salted and aged in jars with rice koji and alcohol. The example we saw begins with tofu set using water from a mud volcano.", "何が起きているか": "What happens?", "麹の酵素が、豆腐のたんぱく質・脂肪・でんぷんをほどいていく。研究では遊離アミノ酸が増え、熟成とともにアルコールやエステルの香りも増える。": "Koji enzymes break down the tofu’s proteins, fats, and starch. Studies show free amino acids increasing, and aromas from alcohols and esters building as aging progresses.", "今回、わかっていないこと": "What we could not establish", "泥火山由来の凝固剤を使った豆腐乳が、通常の豆腐乳と微生物や代謝でどう異なるかは、今回確認できていない。": "We could not confirm how doufuru made with a coagulant from a mud volcano differs from ordinary doufuru in its microbes or metabolism.", "出典をひらく": "Open sources", "18 (2007)。公開前にURLを再照合する。": "18 (2007). URLs to be rechecked before publication.", "×　通常表示へ": "×　Back to standard view", "第2章「土地にあるもので」 / 第1—7章＋終章プロトタイプ v0.11.2": "Chapter 2, “What the Land Provides” / Chapters 1–7 + Epilogue, prototype v0.11.2", "第3章の旅程地図": "Route map for Chapter 3", "第3章の場面を選ぶ": "Choose a scene from Chapter 3", "第3章 値段のないスーパー｜台湾一周・発酵の旅": "Chapter 3: A Supermarket Without Prices | A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "第2章": "Chapter 2", "/　第3章　/": "/　Chapter 3　/", "値段のない<br>スーパー": "A Supermarket<br>Without Prices", "触れて嗅げるけど、買えない。": "You can touch it and smell it, but you can’t buy it.", "この章の場所　台東・樹屋廣場": "Location for this chapter: Tree House Plaza, Taitung", "同じ会場の複数の展示を訪ねた章のため、地点は一つにまとめて表示。": "This chapter covers several exhibitions at the same venue, so they are shown as a single location.", "Sirawの観察ノートをひらく　↓": "Open the notes on siraw　↓", "Siraw<br>塩と時間でつくる肉の保存食": "Siraw<br>Preserving meat with salt and time", "豚肉と塩。花蓮の農業改良場が調べた基本型では、肉の重さの4%の塩をまぶし、一日半から二日後にさらに8%。米飯や酒粕を加える型は、この研究では扱われていない。": "Pork and salt. In the basic method studied by the agricultural research station in Hualien, salt equal to 4% of the meat’s weight is rubbed in, then another 8% is added a day and a half to two days later. The study did not cover versions made with cooked rice or wine lees.", "主な微生物と、起きていること": "The main microorganisms, and what happens", "乳酸菌。農業改良場はSirawそのものを測り、乳酸菌の発酵であることを確かめている。塩が適量だと乳酸菌が増え、酸ができて、ほかの菌が抑えられる。ただし、公開資料から属名までは確認できない。": "Lactic acid bacteria. The research station tested siraw itself and confirmed lactic acid fermentation. With the right amount of salt, lactic acid bacteria multiply, produce acid, and suppress other bacteria. The genera involved, however, cannot be confirmed from published materials.", "まだ誰も見に行っていないこと": "What no one has looked into yet", "塩だけ、肉汁を残す、ご飯を加える、酒粕を加える。四つの作り分けで、菌や味がどう変わるのか。比較した研究は、今回確認できていない。": "Salt alone; retaining the juices; adding rice; adding wine lees. How do the microbes and flavors differ across these four methods? We could find no comparative study.", "農業部花蓮區農業改良場「阿美族醃肉Siraw的參與式研究」／農業部・農業知識入口網「花蓮區農業專訊」。公開前にURLを再照合する。": "Hualien District Agricultural Research and Extension Station, Ministry of Agriculture, “Participatory Research on Amis Fermented Meat, Siraw” (阿美族醃肉Siraw的參與式研究) / “Hualien District Agricultural Bulletin” (花蓮區農業專訊), Ministry of Agriculture’s Agricultural Knowledge Portal. URLs to be rechecked before publication.", "第3章「値段のないスーパー」 / 第1—7章＋終章プロトタイプ v0.11.2": "Chapter 3, “A Supermarket Without Prices” / Chapters 1–7 + Epilogue, prototype v0.11.2", "第4章の旅程地図": "Route map for Chapter 4", "第4章の場面を選ぶ": "Choose a scene from Chapter 4", "第4章 まだ気に入っていません｜台湾一周・発酵の旅": "Chapter 4: “I’m Not Happy with It Yet” | A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "/　第4章　/": "/　Chapter 4　/", "まだ気に<br>入っていません": "“I’m Not Happy<br>with It Yet”", "台湾の南、屏東の田んぼのなかにある醤油蔵、皇珵醬油を訪ねました。": "We visited Royaljade Brewing (皇珵醬油), a soy sauce brewery among the rice fields of Pingtung, in southern Taiwan.", "この章の場所　屏東・皇珵醬油 → 台南": "Location for this chapter: Royaljade Brewing, Pingtung → Tainan", "地点は写真GPSまたは施設所在地に基づく。家庭の食卓は地域表示に留める。": "Locations are based on photo GPS data or the venue’s address. The family meal is shown by area only.", "黒豆蔭油の観察ノートをひらく　↓": "Open the notes on black-soybean yinyou　↓", "第4章「まだ気に入っていません」 / 第1—7章＋終章プロトタイプ v0.11.2": "Chapter 4, “I’m Not Happy with It Yet” / Chapters 1–7 + Epilogue, prototype v0.11.2", "第5章の旅程地図": "Route map for Chapter 5", "第5章の場面を選ぶ": "Choose a scene from Chapter 5", "第5章 紐は宝物｜台湾一周・発酵の旅": "Chapter 5: The String Is a Treasure | A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "/　第5章　/": "/　Chapter 5　/", "だから、うまくいっていた時の紐を、<br>捨てない。": "That is why he keeps the string<br>from the batches that went well.", "この章の場所　新竹・北埔": "Location for this chapter: Beipu, Hsinchu", "地点は地域表示に留める。作業場の正確な位置は公開しない。": "Shown by area only. We do not publish the exact location of the workshop.", "酸柑茶の観察ノートをひらく　↓": "Open the notes on suangan cha　↓", "第5章「紐は宝物」 / 第1—7章＋終章プロトタイプ v0.11.2": "Chapter 5, “The String Is a Treasure” / Chapters 1–7 + Epilogue, prototype v0.11.2", "第6章の旅程地図": "Route map for Chapter 6", "第6章の場面を選ぶ": "Choose a scene from Chapter 6", "第6章 獲らない季節｜台湾一周・発酵の旅": "Chapter 6: The Season of Not Taking | A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "/　第6章　/": "/　Chapter 6　/", "あの晩、<br>順番がひとつ入れ替わりました。": "That evening,<br>something changed order in my mind.", "この章の場所　台北・迪化街": "Location for this chapter: Dihua Street, Taipei", "魚の塩漬けの観察ノートをひらく　↓": "Open the notes on salt-cured fish　↓", "第6章「獲らない季節」 / 第1—7章＋終章プロトタイプ v0.11.2": "Chapter 6 “The Season of Not Taking” / Chapters 1–7 + Epilogue prototype v0.11.2", "第7章の旅程地図": "Chapter 7 route map", "第7章の場面を選ぶ": "Choose a scene from Chapter 7", "第7章 ぬか床を渡す｜台湾一周・発酵の旅": "Chapter 7 Passing On the Nukadoko | A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "/　第7章　/": "/ Chapter 7 /", "手作りを手放さないまま、<br>どこまで大きくできるのか。": "How much can we grow<br>while keeping things handmade?", "この章の場所　台北・大稻埕": "This chapter: Dadaocheng, Taipei", "ぬか床の観察ノートをひらく　↓": "Open the nukadoko observation notes ↓", "第7章「ぬか床を渡す」 / 第1—7章＋終章プロトタイプ v0.11.2": "Chapter 7 “Passing On the Nukadoko” / Chapters 1–7 + Epilogue prototype v0.11.2", "終章の場所": "Epilogue location", "終章の場面を選ぶ": "Choose a scene from the Epilogue", "終章 異文化と繋がるほど、嵐山が深くなる｜台湾一周・発酵の旅": "Epilogue Connecting with Other Cultures, Deepening Our Roots in Arashiyama | A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "/　終章": "/ Epilogue", "異文化と繋がるほど、<br>嵐山が深くなる": "Connecting with Other Cultures,<br>Deepening Our Roots in Arashiyama", "京都に帰ってきてから、<br>まだ考えている。": "Back in Kyoto,<br>I am still thinking.", "この章の場所　京都・嵐山": "This chapter: Arashiyama, Kyoto", "果子狸コーヒーの観察ノートをひらく　↓": "Open the Guozili Coffee observation notes ↓", "終章「異文化と繋がるほど、嵐山が深くなる」 / 第1—7章＋終章プロトタイプ v0.11.2": "Epilogue “Connecting with Other Cultures, Deepening Our Roots in Arashiyama” / Chapters 1–7 + Epilogue prototype v0.11.2", "第1章の旅程地図": "Chapter 1 route map", "第1章の場面を選ぶ": "Choose a scene from Chapter 1", "第1章 南へ南へ運ばれていった｜台湾一周・発酵の旅": "Chapter 1 Carried Further South | A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "第1章　/": "Chapter 1 /", "南へ南へ<br>運ばれていった": "Carried<br>Further South", "この冊子は、発酵文化や知恵が、人から人へ、時間と場所を超えて渡っていく仕組みについての、体感的考察である。": "This booklet is a reflection, grounded in experience, on how the culture and knowledge of fermentation travel from person to person, across time and place.", "この章の旅程　関西空港 → 台北 → 東海岸 → 玉里": "This chapter’s route: Kansai Airport → Taipei → East coast → Yuli", "地点：写真GPSまたは施設所在地に基づく。川辺は私的な位置情報として公開時にぼかす。": "Locations: based on photo GPS data or the addresses of places visited. The riverside location is private and will be blurred on publication.", "臭豆腐の観察ノートをひらく　↓": "Open the stinky tofu observation notes ↓", "臭滷水<br>発酵した漬け汁のこと": "Chou lu shui<br>The fermented soaking liquid", "臭豆腐用の「臭滷水」そのもの。研究には、葉野菜を自然発酵させた滷水や、複数の食材を含むモデル滷水がある。豆腐はこの発酵済み滷水に浸漬される。": "The stinky-tofu brine, chou lu shui, itself. Studies include brines made by naturally fermenting leafy vegetables, and model brines containing several ingredients. The tofu is soaked in this fermented brine.", "臭滷水には乳酸発酵だけでなく、タンパク質・含窒素化合物の分解が関わる。製法や微生物相は、原料・地域・製造所ごとに異なる。": "Chou lu shui involves not only lactic acid fermentation but also the breakdown of proteins and nitrogen-containing compounds. Methods and microbial communities differ with the ingredients, the region, and the maker.", "花蓮・玉里で食べた臭滷水そのものの微生物組成は測定していない。別地域の研究結果を、その店の菌としては扱わない。": "We have not measured the microbial makeup of the brine itself at the shop in Yuli, Hualien. Findings from studies in other regions are not treated as that shop’s microbes.", "123 (2008)。公開前に直接照合済みのURLだけを掲載する。": "123 (2008). Only URLs checked directly before publication are listed.", "第1章「南へ南へ運ばれていった」 / 第1—7章＋終章プロトタイプ v0.11.2": "Chapter 1 “Carried Further South” / Chapters 1–7 + Epilogue prototype v0.11.2", "台湾本島を一周する旅の地図": "Map of the journey around the main island of Taiwan", "台湾一周・発酵の旅": "A Fermentation Journey Around Taiwan", "台湾一周<br>発酵の旅": "A Fermentation Journey<br>Around Taiwan", "京都・嵐山の発酵食堂が、熟成ぬか床をひとつ抱えて、台湾をぐるりと一周した。": "A fermentation restaurant in Arashiyama, Kyoto, carried one mature nukadoko all the way around Taiwan.", "ここから": "Start here", "第1章　花蓮・玉里　8/10": "Chapter 1　Yuli, Hualien　August 10", "第2章　花蓮・羅山村　8/11": "Chapter 2　Luoshan Village, Hualien　August 11", "第3章　台東　8/12": "Chapter 3　Taitung　August 12", "第4章　屏東・皇珵醬油　8/13": "Chapter 4　Royaljade Brewing, Pingtung　August 13", "第5章　新竹・北埔　8/14": "Chapter 5　Beipu, Hsinchu　August 14", "第6章　台北・迪化街　8/15": "Chapter 6　Dihua Street, Taipei　August 15", "第7章　台北・大稻埕　8/16": "Chapter 7　Dadaocheng, Taipei　August 16", "終": "End", "終章　京都・嵐山　帰国後": "Epilogue　Arashiyama, Kyoto　After returning home", "黒豆蔭油<br>甕と天日で熟成する醤油": "Black-Soybean Yinyou<br>Soy sauce aged in jars, in the sun", "黒豆。蒸して麹をつけ、塩を加えて甕に入れ、日に当てる。豆で麹をつくったあと、高塩の環境で時間をかける。": "Black soybeans. They are steamed, grown into koji, mixed with salt, put in jars, and set in the sun. After the koji-making stage, the beans spend a long time in a highly saline environment.", "麹づくりにはコウジカビ。甕のなかでは耐塩性乳酸菌や酵母が関わる。麹の酵素が黒豆のたんぱく質をアミノ酸に、でんぷんを糖にほどき、塩水のなかで酸と香りがつくられる。": "Koji mold is used to make the koji. In the jars, salt-tolerant lactic acid bacteria and yeasts go to work. Koji enzymes break the black soybeans’ proteins down into amino acids and their starch into sugars, and acids and aromas form in the brine.", "甕に入れて天日に置く昔ながらの蔭油と、高温でつくる蔭油で菌の移り変わりが同じかどうか。そして、この蔵の甕のなかは、誰も測っていない。": "Whether microbes follow the same succession in traditional yinyou, set in jars in the sun, as in yinyou made at high temperatures. And no one has tested what is inside this brewery’s jars.", "33 (2013)／國立中興大學「黑豆之蒸煮與機械製麴之研究」。公開前にURLを再照合する。": "33 (2013) / National Chung Hsing University, “A Study of Black-Soybean Steaming and Mechanical Koji Production” (黑豆之蒸煮與機械製麴之研究). URLs to be rechecked before publication.", "酸柑茶<br>発酵しているのか、まだわからない": "Suangan Cha<br>Is it fermenting? We don’t know yet", "虎頭柑の皮と果肉、茶葉。詰め、縛り、蒸して干し、甕で寝かせる。劉先生のお父さんの代までは九回。五代目の劉先生が三回で仕上げる方法を編み出した。": "The peel and flesh of hutougan citrus, and tea leaves. Filled, tied, steamed, dried, and laid to rest in jars. Until Liu’s father’s generation, nine times. Liu, the fifth-generation maker, devised a way to finish it in three.", "わかっていること": "What we know", "長期陳化した酸柑茶では、柑橘由来のフラボノイドが新しいものより少なくなった試料が報告されている。蒸す・干す・寝かせることで成分が変わることは確かめられている。": "Samples of long-aged suangan cha have been reported with lower levels of citrus flavonoids than newer ones. Steaming, drying, and aging have been shown to change its composition.", "まだわかっていないこと": "What we don’t know yet", "酸柑茶そのものの菌を直接調べた研究は、今回確認できていない。その変化が菌の仕事か、熱と時間の仕事か。作り手は發酵と呼ぶが、まだ誰も見に行っていない。": "We could not find any study directly examining the microbes in suangan cha itself. Are the changes the work of microbes, or of heat and time? Its makers call it fajiao, but no one has yet gone to look.", "67(4) (2020)／桃園區農業改良場・柑橘加工資料。公開前にURLを再照合する。": "67(4) (2020) / Taoyuan District Agricultural Research and Extension Station, citrus-processing materials. URLs to be rechecked before publication.", "台東果子狸咖啡<br>腸を、発酵スターターにする": "Taitung Guozili Coffee<br>Turning the gut into a fermentation starter", "旅の本文とは別に、写真の一袋から見えてきた技術を残す。": "Apart from the story of the journey, a record of the technique we found behind one bag in a photograph.", "これは「糞から採ったコーヒー」ではない": "This is not “coffee collected from droppings”", "写真の「台東果子狸咖啡」は、台東・卑南の山豬園農場の商品。果子狸にコーヒーを食べさせ、排泄物から豆を回収する従来方式とは異なる。果子狸の腸内から取り出した菌群を培養し、コーヒー豆を体外で発酵させる。袋にも「もう果子狸に豆を食べさせて排泄物を得る方式はとらない」と書いてある。": "The “Taitung Guozili Coffee” in the photograph is a product of Shanzhuyuan Farm (山豬園農場) in Beinan, Taitung. It differs from the conventional method of feeding coffee to civets and recovering the beans from their droppings. Microbial communities taken from the civets’ guts are cultured, and the coffee beans are fermented outside the body. The bag itself says they no longer feed beans to civets and collect them from the droppings.", "何を再現しているか": "What is being reproduced", "最初の菌だけは、コーヒー豆を食べた果子狸の排泄物から採った。そこから乳酸菌、酵母、資料でB菌・F菌と呼ばれる菌を分離し、発酵時間・菌量・温度を試した。菌の開発だけで約1年半かかったという。以後の製造では培養した菌群を使うため、動物にコーヒーを食べさせる工程はない。": "Only the first microbes were taken from the droppings of civets that had eaten coffee beans. From these, lactic acid bacteria, yeasts, and microbes referred to in the materials as “B bacteria” and “F bacteria” were isolated, and fermentation time, microbial quantity, and temperature were tested. Developing the microbes alone reportedly took about a year and a half. Production since then uses the cultured communities, so no animals are fed coffee.", "発酵を、動物から切り離す": "Separating fermentation from the animal", "野生動物の腸内で起きていた変化を観察し、菌を取り出し、条件を型にして、地域のコーヒーへ返す。工業技術研究院（工研院）南分院が指導し、経済部のSBIR研発経費補助で開発された（袋の表示より）。理由として語られているのは、食品衛生・安全と、規模を広げられること。": "Observe the changes taking place in a wild animal’s gut, extract the microbes, turn the conditions into a method, and give it back to local coffee. It was developed under the guidance of the Southern Region Campus of the Industrial Technology Research Institute (ITRI), with R&D funding from the Ministry of Economic Affairs’ SBIR program (according to the bag). The reasons given are food hygiene and safety, and the ability to scale up.", "呼び方に残る注意": "A caution about the name", "パッケージの英語は Formosan Masked Civet。インドネシアのコピ・ルアクで知られるアジアパームシベットとは別の、台湾の果子狸（ハクビシン）を指す。だからこれは「台湾版コピ・ルアク」より、「台湾の果子狸の腸内菌から着想した発酵コーヒー」と呼ぶほうが近い。": "The English on the package says Formosan Masked Civet. This is Taiwan’s guozili, the masked palm civet, distinct from the Asian palm civet known from Indonesia’s kopi luwak. So rather than “Taiwan’s kopi luwak,” it is closer to call it “a fermented coffee inspired by the gut microbes of Taiwan’s masked palm civet.”", "袋の表示（輔導単位：工業技術研究院南分院／研発経費：経済部技術処SBIR／産学合作：国立台東大学）／工商時報 2010年9月17日「5隻果子狸 讓台東咖啡全台飄香」／工研院「工業技術與資訊」月刊「體外發酵新商機 台東果子狸咖啡飄香」／池上郷農会「台東果子狸咖啡」商品ページ": "Bag labeling (guidance: ITRI Southern Region Campus / R&D funding: SBIR, Department of Industrial Technology, Ministry of Economic Affairs / industry–academia collaboration: National Taitung University) / Commercial Times, September 17, 2010, “5隻果子狸 讓台東咖啡全台飄香” / ITRI monthly 工業技術與資訊, “體外發酵新商機 台東果子狸咖啡飄香” / Chishang Farmers’ Association, “台東果子狸咖啡” product page", "臭滷水（しゅうろすい）": "Chou lu shui", "豆腐ではなく、漬け汁のほう。台湾北部の店を調べた研究では、葉野菜を自然発酵させた汁だった。店によって中身は違う。豆腐は、できあがった汁に浸けられる。": "The soaking liquid, not the tofu. A study of shops in northern Taiwan found that the liquid was made by naturally fermenting leafy vegetables. Ingredients vary from shop to shop. The tofu is soaked in the finished brine.", "主な微生物": "The main microorganisms", "台湾の臭滷水から、乳酸菌が7属32種見つかっている（Lactobacillus、Enterococcus、Weissella など）。枯草菌の仲間（Bacillus）も。": "Thirty-two species of lactic acid bacteria, from seven genera, have been found in Taiwanese stinky-tofu brine, including Lactobacillus, Enterococcus, and Weissella. Bacillus bacteria have also been found.", "乳酸菌が働くだけではない。たんぱく質が分解されてアンモニアが増え、汁はアルカリに傾く。あの匂いは、そこから来る。": "Lactic acid bacteria are not the only organisms at work. As proteins break down, ammonia increases and the liquid becomes more alkaline. That is where the smell comes from.", "玉里のあの店の汁は、誰も測っていない。どの菌がどの匂いをどれだけ作っているかも、まだ十分にはわかっていない。": "No one has tested the brine at that particular shop in Yuli. Nor do we yet fully understand which microbes produce which smells, and in what quantities.", "出典：Chao et al., International Journal of Food Microbiology 123 (2008)／Taiwan FDA, Journal of Food and Drug Analysis": "Sources: Chao et al., International Journal of Food Microbiology 123 (2008) / Taiwan FDA, Journal of Food and Drug Analysis", "豆腐乳": "Doufuru", "大豆の豆腐。塩漬けにして、米麹や酒と一緒に瓶で熟成させる。": "Tofu made from soybeans. It is salted and aged in jars with rice koji and alcohol.", "台湾式の製法を調べた台湾大学の研究では、麹づくりにコウジカビ（Aspergillus oryzae）。市販品から枯草菌の仲間（Bacillus）も見つかっているが、主役かどうかはわかっていない。": "A National Taiwan University study of the Taiwanese method used the koji mold Aspergillus oryzae to make koji. Bacillus bacteria have also been found in commercial products, though whether they play a central role is unclear.", "麹の酵素が、豆腐のたんぱく質・脂肪・でんぷんをほどいていく。研究では16日間で、たんぱく質の12〜20%がアミノ酸になった。熟成が進むと、アルコールやエステルの香りが増える。「中華のチーズ」と呼ばれる理由は、ここにある。": "Koji enzymes break down the tofu's proteins, fats, and starch. In one study, 12–20% of the protein was converted into amino acids over sixteen days. As aging progresses, aromas from alcohols and esters increase. This helps explain why doufuru is called \"Chinese cheese.\"", "泥豆腐の豆腐乳と、普通の木綿豆腐の豆腐乳で、発酵の仕方や微生物の種類が違うかは、まだ誰も見に行っていない。": "Whether doufuru made from mud-volcano tofu ferments differently, or with different microbes, from doufuru made from ordinary firm tofu: no one has gone to look yet.", "出典：National Taiwan University, \"Changes of enzyme activity and physiochemical properties during the fermentation of Taiwanese style sufu\"／Kung et al., Food Control 18 (2007)": "Sources: National Taiwan University, “Changes of enzyme activity and physiochemical properties during the fermentation of Taiwanese style sufu” / Kung et al., Food Control 18 (2007)", "出典：National Taiwan University,": "Sources: National Taiwan University,", "豚肉と塩。花蓮の農業改良場が調べた基本型では、肉の重さの4%の塩をまぶし、一日半から二日後にさらに8%。計12%ほど。米飯や酒粕を加える型は、この研究では扱われていない。": "Pork and salt. In the basic method studied by the agricultural research station in Hualien, salt equal to 4% of the meat's weight is rubbed in, then another 8% is added a day and a half to two days later—about 12% in total. The study did not cover versions made with cooked rice or wine lees.", "乳酸菌。農業改良場が実物を測り、乳酸菌の発酵であることを確かめている。菌は仕込むのではなく、その場の環境から入ってくる。ただし、どの属の乳酸菌かは公開されていない。": "Lactic acid bacteria. The research station tested siraw itself and confirmed lactic acid fermentation. The bacteria come from the surrounding environment rather than being deliberately added. The genera involved, however, have not been disclosed.", "塩が適量だと乳酸菌が増え、酸ができて、ほかの菌が抑えられる。塩が多すぎると乳酸菌も増えられない。少なすぎると大腸菌などを抑えられない。12%は、その間を探した数字。": "With the right amount of salt, lactic acid bacteria multiply, produce acid, and suppress other bacteria. Too much salt prevents even the lactic acid bacteria from growing. Too little fails to suppress organisms such as E. coli. The figure of 12% came from seeking a balance between the two.", "塩だけ、肉汁を残す、ご飯を加える、酒粕を加える。四つの作り分けで、菌や味がどう変わるのか。比べた研究はまだない。": "Salt alone; retaining the juices; adding rice; adding wine lees. How do the microbes and flavors differ across these four methods? There is no comparative study yet.", "出典：農業部花蓮區農業改良場「阿美族醃肉Siraw的參與式研究」／農業部・農業知識入口網「花蓮區農業專訊」": "Sources: Hualien District Agricultural Research and Extension Station, Ministry of Agriculture, \"Participatory Research on Amis Fermented Meat, Siraw\" (阿美族醃肉Siraw的參與式研究) / \"Hualien District Agricultural Bulletin\" (花蓮區農業專訊), Ministry of Agriculture's Agricultural Knowledge Portal", "黒豆蔭油（インヨウ）": "Black-Soybean Yinyou", "黒豆。蒸して麹をつけ、塩を加えて甕に入れ、封をして日に当てる。豆で麹をつくったあと、塩の濃い環境で時間をかける。": "Black soybeans. They are steamed, grown into koji, mixed with salt, sealed in jars, and placed in the sun. After the koji-making stage, the beans spend a long time in a highly saline environment.", "麹づくりにコウジカビ（Aspergillus）。甕のなかでは、塩に強い乳酸菌（Tetragenococcus など）が働く。台湾の蔭油を直接調べた研究で、Tetragenococcus、Enterococcus、Weissella、Bacillus などが見つかっている。台湾の食品工業発展研究所には、蔭油から採った Tetragenococcus halophilus の株が保存されている。": "Aspergillus molds are used to make the koji. In the jars, salt-tolerant lactic acid bacteria, including Tetragenococcus, go to work. Studies of Taiwanese yinyou itself have found Tetragenococcus, Enterococcus, Weissella, and Bacillus, among others. Taiwan's Food Industry Research and Development Institute maintains a strain of Tetragenococcus halophilus isolated from yinyou.", "麹の酵素が黒豆のたんぱく質をアミノ酸に、でんぷんを糖にほどく。塩水のなかで乳酸菌が酸をつくり、塩に強い酵母がアルコールと香りをつくる。": "Koji enzymes break the black soybeans' proteins down into amino acids and their starch into sugars. In the brine, lactic acid bacteria produce acids, while salt-tolerant yeasts produce alcohol and aroma compounds.", "甕に入れて天日に置く昔ながらの蔭油と、工場で温度を上げてつくる蔭油で、菌の移り変わりが同じかどうか。そして、この蔵の甕のなかは、誰も測っていない。": "Whether microbes follow the same succession in traditional, sun-aged yinyou as in factory-made yinyou fermented at raised temperatures. And no one has tested what is happening inside this brewery's jars.", "出典：Wei et al., Food Microbiology 33 (2013)／國立中興大學「黑豆之蒸煮與機械製麴之研究」": "Sources: Wei et al., Food Microbiology 33 (2013) / National Chung Hsing University, \"A Study of Black-Soybean Steaming and Mechanical Koji Production\" (黑豆之蒸煮與機械製麴之研究)", "酸柑茶<br>発酵とは何か。まだ考えている。": "Suangan Cha<br>What is fermentation? We are still thinking about it.", "虎頭柑（フートウガン）という柑橘の皮と果肉、茶葉。果肉を出して茶と混ぜ、皮に戻し、縛って、蒸す・干す・押すを繰り返す。劉先生のお父さんの代までは九回（九蒸九曬）。五代目の劉先生が、三回で仕上げる方法を編み出した。そのあと甕で寝かせる。": "The peel and flesh of a citrus fruit called hutougan, together with tea leaves. The flesh is scooped out, mixed with tea, returned to the peel, and tied up. Steaming, drying, and pressing are repeated. Until Liu's father's generation, the cycle was repeated nine times—\"nine steamings, nine sun-dryings.\" Liu, the fifth-generation maker, devised a method requiring three. The tea is then aged in jars.", "わかっていない。酸柑茶そのものの菌を調べた研究が見つからない。名前を挙げない。": "Unknown. We could find no study examining the microbes in suangan cha itself, so we do not name any here.", "皮のフラボノイド（hesperidin、narirutin）が、長く寝かせたものでは新しいものの1〜3割まで減っていた、という中興大学の報告がある。蒸す・干す・寝かせるで、皮の成分が変わることは確かめられている。それが菌の仕事なのか、熱と時間の仕事なのかは、わかっていない。": "A National Chung Hsing University report found that flavonoids in the peel—hesperidin and narirutin—had fallen in long-aged samples to 10–30% of the levels in newer ones. Steaming, drying, and aging have been shown to change the peel's chemical composition. Whether microbes are responsible, or heat and time, remains unknown.", "酸柑茶は、微生物の意味で「発酵」しているのか。作り手は發酵と呼ぶ。蒸すことも干すことも、ふつうは殺菌の工程。菌がいないと確かめられたのではなく、誰も見に行っていない。\n三回蒸す、という形には、似たものが二つある。一つは納豆。稲わらを95℃で5分処理すると、熱に耐える芽胞をつくる菌だけが残る、という研究がある。もう一つは微生物学の「間欠滅菌」。100℃で加熱し、一晩置いて芽胞を発芽させ、また加熱する。これを三日繰り返す。こちらは芽胞を残すためでなく、発芽させて殺すための手法。酸柑茶の三回が、納豆の側なのか、間欠滅菌の側なのか、それとも菌ではなく熱と時間の仕事なのか。蒸す・干すの途中の菌を測った研究は、見つからなかった。": "Is suangan cha \"fermented\" in the microbial sense? Its makers call it fajiao. Steaming and drying are usually understood as processes that kill microbes. Microbes have not been shown to be absent; no one has yet looked.\nThe three steamings bring two other processes to mind. One is natto: a study found that treating rice straw at 95°C for five minutes leaves only bacteria that form heat-resistant spores. The other is intermittent sterilization in microbiology: heat to 100°C, leave overnight for spores to germinate, then heat again. Repeat over three days. This is a method for making spores germinate and then killing the resulting cells, rather than preserving them. Are suangan cha's three steamings closer to natto-making, to intermittent sterilization, or to a process driven by heat and time rather than microbes? We found no study measuring the microbes between the steaming and drying stages.", "酸柑茶は、微生物の意味で「発酵」しているのか。作り手は發酵と呼ぶ。蒸すことも干すことも、ふつうは殺菌の工程。菌がいないと確かめられたのではなく、誰も見に行っていない。": "Is suangan cha \"fermented\" in the microbial sense? Its makers call it fajiao. Steaming and drying are usually understood as processes that kill microbes. Microbes have not been shown to be absent; no one has yet looked.", "三回蒸す、という形には、似たものが二つある。一つは納豆。稲わらを95℃で5分処理すると、熱に耐える芽胞をつくる菌だけが残る、という研究がある。もう一つは微生物学の「間欠滅菌」。100℃で加熱し、一晩置いて芽胞を発芽させ、また加熱する。これを三日繰り返す。こちらは芽胞を残すためでなく、発芽させて殺すための手法。酸柑茶の三回が、納豆の側なのか、間欠滅菌の側なのか、それとも菌ではなく熱と時間の仕事なのか。蒸す・干すの途中の菌を測った研究は、見つからなかった。": "The three steamings bring two other processes to mind. One is natto: a study found that treating rice straw at 95°C for five minutes leaves only bacteria that form heat-resistant spores. The other is intermittent sterilization in microbiology: heat to 100°C, leave overnight for spores to germinate, then heat again. Repeat over three days. This is a method for making spores germinate and then killing the resulting cells, rather than preserving them. Are suangan cha's three steamings closer to natto-making, to intermittent sterilization, or to a process driven by heat and time rather than microbes? We found no study measuring the microbes between the steaming and drying stages.", "出典：Kao et al., J. Agric. For. 67(4) (2020)／桃園區農業改良場 柑橘加工資料／Kubo et al., Appl. Environ. Microbiol. (2011) 稲わらの芽胞形成菌／柑普茶の菌叢 Integrative Biology 53(3) (2024)": "Sources: Kao et al., J. Agric. For. 67(4) (2020) / Taoyuan District Agricultural Research and Extension Station, citrus-processing materials / Kubo et al., Appl. Environ. Microbiol. (2011), on spore-forming bacteria in rice straw / A study of microbial communities in ganpu citrus pu-erh tea, Integrative Biology 53(3) (2024)", "泰雅族の魚の塩漬け<br>集落のやり方は、まだ誰も測っていない": "Atayal Salt-Cured Fish<br>No one has yet measured how the village does it", "川魚。リーダーの説明では、塩をまぶして台所で漬ける。清華大学の記録にある泰雅族の「醃苦花魚」では、魚を塩で1〜2日漬けたあと、冷ました米飯と交互に容器へ詰めて密閉する。暑い時期で半月、寒い時期で一か月。文献では米飯も一緒に発酵する。リーダーの集落のやり方と同じかどうかは、わからない。": "River fish. The leader described coating it with salt and curing it in the kitchen. In a National Tsing Hua University account of Atayal cured kuhua fish (醃苦花魚), the fish are salted for one or two days, then packed in alternating layers with cooled cooked rice and sealed. Fermentation takes about half a month in warm weather, a month in cold weather. In that account, the rice ferments alongside the fish. We do not know whether this is the same method used in the leader's village.", "わかっていない。泰雅族の醃魚そのものを調べた研究が見つからない。名前を挙げない。": "Unknown. We could find no study examining Atayal cured fish itself, so we do not name any here.", "文献の説明では、米飯が自然に発酵して酸をつくり、塩漬けの魚が酸性になって、塩と酸の両方で保存が効く。機器で測った研究ではない。": "According to the written account, the rice ferments naturally and produces acid, acidifying the salted fish. Both salt and acid help preserve it. This account is not based on instrument measurements.", "集落ごとに、塩だけの漬け方と、米飯を入れる漬け方があるのかどうか。「中くらいがいちばんいい」のはなぜか——身の硬さと漬かる時間の関係を、誰も測っていない。": "Whether some villages cure fish with salt alone while others add cooked rice. And why “medium-sized ones are best”: no one has measured the relationship between firmness of flesh and curing time.", "出典：傅麗玉（國立清華大學）「泰雅族的醃苦花魚」『臺灣化學教育』／原住民族文化事業基金會資料": "Sources: Fu Li-yu, National Tsing Hua University, “Atayal Cured Kuhua Fish” (泰雅族的醃苦花魚), Taiwan Chemistry Education / Materials from the Indigenous Peoples Cultural Foundation", "ぬか床<br>床ごとに、菌の顔ぶれが違う": "Nukadoko<br>The microbial cast differs from bed to bed", "米ぬかと水と塩。野菜を繰り返し漬け、ぬかを足しながら床を保つ。米ぬかの糖と、野菜から持ち込まれるものが餌になる。": "Rice bran, water, and salt. Vegetables are pickled in the bed again and again, and more bran is added to maintain it. Sugars in the bran, together with material brought in by the vegetables, feed the microorganisms.", "日本のぬか床を直接調べた研究が複数ある。長く続いた床では乳酸菌（Lactobacillus 属）が主。新しく起こした床では、最後に Lactiplantibacillus plantarum が優勢になる例。近年の研究では Loigolactobacillus が7〜8割を占めた床もあった。床ごとに、菌の顔ぶれが違う。": "Several studies have examined Japanese nukadoko directly. Long-maintained beds are dominated by lactic acid bacteria of the genus Lactobacillus. In some newly started beds, Lactiplantibacillus plantarum eventually becomes dominant. A recent study found beds in which Loigolactobacillus accounted for 70–80% of the community. The microbial cast differs from bed to bed.", "起こしはじめの床では pH が6.5から4.0〜4.2へ下がり、乳酸が増える。遊離アミノ酸も増える。酸と塩と、菌どうしの競り合いで、野菜が漬かる。": "In a newly started bed, pH falls from 6.5 to 4.0–4.2, and lactic acid increases. Free amino acids also increase. Acid, salt, and competition among the microbes work together to pickle the vegetables.", "「ぬか床＝この菌」という一種類の答えはない。原料・土地・野菜・温度・混ぜ方・年数で顔ぶれが変わる。京都から台湾へ渡ったカモシカの床そのものは、測ったことがない。台湾の台所で一年過ごしたぬか床が、京都のぬか床とどう違っていくのか、もしくは同じように熟成するのか、まだ誰も確かめていない。": "There is no single answer to “Which microbe makes nukadoko?” The ingredients, place, vegetables, temperature, mixing, and age all change the community. Kamoshika’s own bed, carried from Kyoto to Taiwan, has never been tested. How a bed that has spent a year in a Taiwanese kitchen will come to differ from one in Kyoto—or whether it will mature in the same way—no one has checked yet.", "出典：Sakamoto et al., Int. J. Food Microbiol. 144 (2011)／Biosci. Microbiota Food Health (2024) PMC11444858": "Sources: Sakamoto et al., Int. J. Food Microbiol. 144 (2011) / Biosci. Microbiota Food Health (2024), PMC11444858", "← 全体地図": "← Full map", "富里郷": "Fuli Township", "發酵迷": "Fajiaomi", "花蓮": "Hualien", "屏東": "Pingtung", "新竹": "Hsinchu", "内湖": "Neihu", "スペース　次の写真<br>← →　前後の場面<br>M　地図へもどる": "Space   Next photo<br>← →   Previous / next scene<br>M   Back to map", "今日の発酵食品　／": "Today’s Ferment /"}, "frag": [["今日の発酵食品　／", "Today’s Ferment /"], ["← 全体地図", "← Full map"], ["富里郷", "Fuli Township"], ["發酵迷", "Fajiaomi"], ["花蓮", "Hualien"], ["屏東", "Pingtung"], ["新竹", "Hsinchu"], ["内湖", "Neihu"]]};
